\par\par \b \uc2 European Morning Briefing: Markets Seen Lower Ahead of U.S. Jobs Data\b0\par\par\uc2 3,215 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2014\par 06:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SNAPSHOT:\par \par \uc2 ========\par \par \uc2 European bourses seen lower, bunds and gilts mixed; euro little changed, spot gold lower, oil up\par \par \uc2 -Crimea Sets Secession Vote as Crisis Escalates\par \par \uc2 -Ukraine's PM Urges Russia to Start Talks, Reject Crimea's Referendum\par \par \uc2 -U.S., EU Impose Sanctions against Russia\par \par \uc2 -BOE Governor to Respond in Parliament to \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  Market Charges -Report\par \par \uc2 -\uc2 Schneider Electric\uc2  Revives Plans to Sell CST Unit -Report\par \par \uc2 -\uc2 Total SA\uc2  Explores Sale of its TotalGaz Subsidiary -Report\par \par \uc2 -Cerberus, Safeway Reach $9 Billion Deal\par \par \uc2 -Fed's Dudley Upbeat on Economy\par \par \uc2 WATCH FOR: Germany industrial output; U.S. trade data, labor data\par \par \uc2 EUROPEAN OUTLOOK & U.S./ASIAN SUMMARIES:\par \par \uc2 ========\par \par \uc2 Investors will shift their radar to the U.S. jobs numbers, hoping a strong number can keep the rally in stocks going. Stock markets may start lower and premium government bonds mixed ahead of the data.\par \par \uc2 Asian markets were mixed on Friday. While the crisis in Ukraine is still to be resolved, investors are focused on economic fundamentals, with confidence boosted by upbeat comments on the eurozone from the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  (ECB), while Wall Street saw another record close.\par \par \uc2 President Barack Obama laid down a tough challenge to Moscow's support for a referendum in Ukraine's Crimea on joining Russia. "The proposed referendum on the future of Crimea would violate the Ukrainian constitution and violate international law," Mr. Obama said.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  formally warned banks Thursday to watch out for transactions by ousted Ukraine President Viktor Yanukovych and his relatives and associates, citing sanctions by Canadian and European officials.\par \par \uc2 Wall Street futures and Treasurys were little changed Friday, with the euro barely moved, spot gold higher and oil higher.\par \par \uc2 STOCKS:\par \par \uc2 ======\par \par \uc2 As the Ukraine crisis festers in the background, European markets were likely to start slightly lower ahead of the U.S. payrolls.\par \par \uc2 Ahead of the opening, IG has called the FTSE down 8 at 6780, the DAX off 15 at 9527, and the CAC off 4 at 4413.\par \par \uc2 Wall Street futures were little changed Friday, after the S&P 500 index closed at a record high after an upbeat reading on weekly jobless claims, while weakness in shares of drug makers helped push the Nasdaq Composite lower.\par \par \uc2 Market participants appeared to be selling some strong performers ahead of the government's February employment report due Friday, according to Rick Fier, director of equity trading at Conifer Securities.\par \par \uc2 The S&P 500 has rallied 5.8% over the past month despite a string of lackluster reports on U.S. manufacturing and housing. Each of the past two monthly jobs reports have missed forecasts, and many have pinned the recent poor economic performance on the cold weather.\par \par \uc2 Friday's payrolls report is eagerly awaited. Economists expect nonfarm payrolls increased by 152,000 in February, versus 113,000 in January. The unemployment rate is expected to tick down one-tenth of one percent to 6.5%. One component, government jobs, will be watched to see if the protracted wave of layoffs of civil servants is letting up. In January, 29,000 government jobs were eliminated.\par \par \uc2 Joseph Quinlan, chief market strategist for U.S. Trust, said that stocks have been rising in recent weeks because investors anticipate a "strong snapback in growth" after winter ends, particularly from consumers. Stocks are likely to rise should Friday's jobs report come in close to expectations, but investors will likely brush off a disappointing report and attribute any softness to the weather, he said.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 :\par \par \uc2 ======\par \par \uc2 The euro was little changed in Asia, following its rally to its highest level against the dollar this year, after the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  decided to leave monetary policy and interest rates unchanged.\par \par \uc2 "On current information, they seem pretty far away from easing," said Jens Nordvig, head of fixed income research Americas and global head of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at Nomura Securities.\par \par \uc2 The yen struggled, particularly against the euro and the dollar, following upbeat jobless claims data in the U.S. and the results of the ECB meeting.\par \par \uc2 A broad expectation remains that U.S. data should improve as the weather warms and the drag and uncertainty surrounding the "weather effect" on the numbers dissipate, according to Richard Cochinos, head of Americas G-10 \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at \uc2 Citigroup\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "For now, better U.S. data means higher risk appetite--and potentially underperformance of the dollar (against) the New Zealand dollar and the euro," Mr. Cochinos wrote in a research report. "A broadly stronger dollar would come later in the month when U.S. data accelerates and the strength of U.S. outperformance becomes clear."\par \par \uc2 BONDS:\par \par \uc2 =====\par \par \uc2 Bunds may start mixed as investors focus on U.S. jobs data after the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's unexciting stance.\par \par \uc2 "In the end, the ECB's March policy meeting seems a bit of a damp squib with no move on interest rates or liquidity, and no strong indication that it feels action is necessary as things stand," said Howard Archer, chief U.K. and European economist at IHS Global Insight in emailed comments.\par \par \uc2 German industrial production is seen rising by 0.7% on the month, according to a Dow Jones poll of analysts.\par \par \uc2 Treasury bonds were flat Friday after markets dropped Thursday as better-than-expected data on weekly jobless claims buoyed sentiment and weakened demand for government debt. The claims data fueled a whisper figure of up to 185,000 for payrolls Friday.\par \par \uc2 Treasury bonds took an additional hit as selling of \uc2 German government\uc2  bonds, another major safe-haven market, spilled over, traders said. ECB President Mario Draghi suggested the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would take its time before adding more stimulus amid signs of improvement in the euro zone's economy.\par \par \uc2 As for U.S. payrolls Friday, "A number that is higher than expected could do some real damage to the Treasury market," said Alex Manzara, vice president of rate futures at brokerage \uc2 R.J. O'Brien & Associates\uc2  in Chicago.\par \par \uc2 New York Fed President William Dudley said recent economic weakness hasn't changed his upbeat expectations for the economy and isn't enough to alter the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's plan to reduce bond purchases.\par \par \uc2 ENERGY:\par \par \uc2 =======\par \par \uc2 Crude-oil futures extended gains in Asia. "Looking ahead, we expect markets to ebb and flow on geopolitical news, but increasingly factor physical indicators," ANZ said. "With northern hemisphere refineries starting to transition to summer fuels in the coming weeks, we expect slightly weaker performance across both benchmarks," the bank said. The U.S. labor market report due later Friday will also provide cues for oil prices. "Payrolls are on tap and markets have been giving every piece of lousy data a free pass to the Bahamas," DBS said.\par \par \uc2 Nymex crude was up 14 cents at $101.70/bbl, and Brent crude was up 16 cents at $108.26/bbl.\par \par \uc2 METALS:\par \par \uc2 ======\par \par \uc2 Gold prices were moving sideways in tight ranges in Asia ahead of U.S. jobs data. A weak figure could suggest that economic recovery remains fragile and underpin gold, which is considered a safe-haven asset. Spot gold prices rose 1.2% overnight after U.S. President Barack Obama said that Crimea's referendum on seceding from Ukraine to join Russia is illegal. He added that the U.S. and EU are united against Russia's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Spot gold was at $1,349.51, down $1.31.\par \par \uc2 Base metals could remain rangebound as traders wait for the payrolls. While weak jobs data could weigh on prices, an upbeat figure could improve market sentiment about demand.\par \par \uc2 LME 3-month copper was $20 higher at $7,050.\par \par \uc2 Write to Dennis Baker at dennis.baker@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 7 Mar 2014 00:00 EDT  European Morning Briefing: DJ Dominant Headlines; Other Top Stories\par \par \uc2 DJ Dominant Headlines:\par \par \uc2 With Shouts and Hugs, Sprint Boss Masayoshi Son of SoftBank Drives Turnaround\par \par \uc2 Tap Oil Seeks to Sell Stakes in 4 Australia Oil, Gas Blocks\par \par \uc2 Merrill Lynch Sees Management Turnover\par \par \uc2 Indonesian Billionaire, U.S. Bondholders in Rescue Talks for Vanadium Miner\par \par \uc2 FAA Exploring Limited Commercial Drone Use\par \par \uc2 Other Top Stories:\par \par \uc2 Shanghai Chaori in Default on Bond Interest Payments\par \par \uc2 A Chinese solar-equipment maker on Friday failed to meet interest payments on a bond, according to an official there, becoming China's first domestic corporate-bond default.\par \par \uc2 Japan Government Says Bitcoin Isn't A \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 The Japanese government officially said Friday that bitcoin is not a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and will not be regulated as a financial product.\par \par \uc2 Minerals Technologies\uc2  Again Boosts Bid for AMCOL\par \par \uc2 Amcol International Corp\uc2 . said it intends to terminate a merger agreement struck with \uc2 Imerys\uc2 , calling a rival bid by \uc2 Minerals Technologies Inc\uc2 . a superior offer for the U.S. bentonite producer.\par \par \uc2 Workers Continue To Strike At An \uc2 IBM\uc2  Factory In China\par \par \uc2 Hundreds of workers at an \uc2 International Business Machines Corp\uc2 . plant struck for a fourth day as the company prepares to transfer the computer-server factory to \uc2 Lenovo Group Ltd\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Cerberus Agrees to Buy Safeway for $9 Billion\par \par \uc2 Cerberus Capital Management LP\uc2  has agreed to buy \uc2 Safeway Inc\uc2 . for more than $9 billion, in what would be the private-equity firm's latest acquisition of a big U.S. grocery chain.\par \par \uc2 'Period of Stability' for Australia Interest Rates -- RBA's Stevens\par \par \uc2 Australia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor gave the strongest hint yet that interest rates would likely remain at a record-low 2.5% for much of the year to help fight rising unemployment.\par \par \uc2 7 Mar 2014 00:00 EDT  European Morning Briefing: Market Prices, Data Calendar\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par DJIA    16421.89 +61.71 +0.38% \par Nasdaq   4352.13  -5.85 -0.13% \par S&P 500  1877.03  +3.22 +0.17% \par FTSE 100 6788.49 +13.07 +0.19% \par DAX      9542.87  +0.85 +0.01% \par CAC40    4417.04 +25.79 +0.59% \par Closing \par  \par Dow Future   16420.00 +5.00 0.0% \par NASDAQ Future 3722.50 +0.50 0.0% \par S&P Future    1877.00 +0.70 0.0% \par As of 0430 GMT \par  \par Nikkei       15195.02  +60.27 +0.40% \par Hang Seng    22661.25  -41.72 -0.18% \par S&P/ASX 200   5456.00  +10.10 +0.19% \par MumbaiSensex 21652.42 +