\par\par \b \uc2 Gold Sideways as Traders Await U.S. Jobs Data -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,610 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2014\par 03:17\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0216 GMT [Dow Jones] Gold prices are moving sideways in a tight range as traders wait from nonfarm payrolls data from the U.S., scheduled later in the global trading day. A weak figure could suggest that economic recovery remains fragile and underpin gold, which is considered a safe-haven asset. Spot gold prices rose 1.2% overnight after U.S. President Barack Obama said that Crimea's referendum on seceding from Ukraine to join Russia is illegal and added that the U.S. and \f28 \uc2 \f2 European Union\f28 \uc2 \f2  are united against Russia's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ukraine\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . Geopolitical tensions in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ukraine\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  have underpinned gold prices this week. Spot gold is at $1,350/oz, down 40 cents from its previous close. (arpan.mukherjee@wsj.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0206 GMT [Dow Jones] China shares are higher with petroleum and petrochemical companies leading gains on hopes that Beijing will push forward state-owned enterprise reforms in the sector, say analysts. The Shanghai Composite Index is up 0.8% at 2075.22 and is tipped to face immediate resistance at 2100. "Bleak economic growth outlook and concerns over bond default risks may rein in the upside in the stock market," says Huang Cendong, an analyst with Sinolink. Among actives, Sinopec (600028.SH) jumped 5.2% to CNY5.49 and \uc2 PetroChina\uc2  (601857.SH) is 2.7% higher to CNY7.88. Metal firms rise on the back of stronger metal prices in the global market; \uc2 Inner Mongolia Baotou Steel Rare-Earth (Group) Hi-Tech\uc2  (600111.SH) gains 2.7% to CNY21.35 and Chalco (601600.SH) climbs 2.1% to CNY3.37. The Shenzhen Composite Index is up 0.5% at 1111.29. (amy.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0206 GMT [Dow Jones] Consumer price inflation in China likely softened in February, economists say. The CPI likely rose just 2.0% on year--down from 2.5% in January, according to the median forecast of 13 economists surveyed by The Wall Street Journal. That remains easily within the maximum tolerance of 3.5% announced by Premier Li Keqiang on Wednesday. "A relatively warm winter should have kept food prices and overall CPI growth soft," \uc2 UBS\uc2  economist Tao Wang says. Meanwhile, the producer price index, which tracks factory-gate prices, may also have taken a turn south. The PPI likely dropped by 1.8% on year in February compared with 1.6% the month before, according to the same survey. (richard.silk@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0204 GMT [Dow Jones] BMD crude palm oil is off to a bullish start at MYR2,890-MYR2,900/ton after finding support near the MRY2,800/ton zone Thursday. Support also comes from soyoil, which posted strong gains of nearly 2% overnight, says Malaysia's AmFutures. "(BMD CPO) is backed fundamentally by anticipations of slower productions and diminishing stockpiles amid dry weather in the plantation states," notes another Kuala Lumpur-based brokerage. Benchmark May CPO settled 1.2% higher at MYR2,867/ton Thursday. (huileng.tan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0201 GMT [Dow Jones] A lesson many countries learned from the Asian financial crisis is that foreign capital can evaporate overnight. As a result, they have amassed \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves as shock absorbers against sudden capital outflows. But not all are so well-prepared for another crisis. \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  says that within emerging markets, Turkey, South Africa and Indonesia are most vulnerable to a \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  crisis. By comparing a country's reserves against its projected external financing needs, the U.S. bank finds that many Asian countries like China, Thailand and South Korea are relatively safe. Goldman estimates the projected financing needs as the sum of the country's expected current account deficit and external repayments coming due. (jacky.wong@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0156 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan shares are up 0.8% at 8779.28 as investors are cheered by \uc2 Taiwan's Financial Supervisory Commission\uc2 's comments that it is mulling measures to stimulate the stock market, says Hua Nan Securities assistant vice president, Henry Miao. TSMC (2330.TW) continues its climb on local media reports that it has begun production of chips for Apple, with its stock up 1.8% at NT$115.00. Other tech heavyweights also advance--Hon Hai (2317.TW) gains 0.7% at NT$85.00 and UMC (2303.TW) is 0.8% higher at NT$12.80. The Taiex is tipped for a 8750-8800 band for the session. (eva.dou@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0149 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/MYR is headed for 3.2000 as two bearish chart indicators concur that the dollar is going down in the near term. The USD/MYR has dropped below its daily Ichimoku Cloud support zone and is also within its daily Bollinger downtrend channel, adding to bearish technical bias. The dollar was hammered overnight by the sharp jump in the euro after the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  kept its base interest rate unchanged. The improvement in U.S. initial jobless claims also gave investors added confidence, helping the S&P 500 stock index to hit a new record high. The overall boost to investor confidence will help the ringgit and other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  rally further. \uc2 Malaysia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Thursday kept the benchmark interest rate unchanged at 3.00% as expected. USD/MYR is now 3.2540 versus its Thursday close of 3.2570. (ewen.chew@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0148 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/SGD has broken its daily Ichimoku Cloud support at 1.2643 and is now targeting entry into the Bollinger downtrend channel that begins at 1.2615. The overnight leap of the euro, triggered by the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  keeping its base interest rate unchanged, has pummelled the U.S. dollar across the board. Also helping Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to gain was the improvement in U.S. initial jobless claims that gave more confidence to global investors that a U.S. economic recovery is gathering steam. If USD/SGD ends Friday below 1.2615 and thereby within the downtrend channel, the Singapore dollar could accelerate higher toward 1.2500 next week. USD/SGD is now 1.2633 versus its Thursday close of 1.2631. (ewen.chew@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0148 GMT [Dow Jones] Crude-oil futures extend gains in early Asian trade Friday after U.S. financial markets ended higher overnight and with events in Ukraine affecting oil market sentiment. The Moscow-backed Crimean government has called for a referendum in 10 days to ratify its decision to secede from Ukraine and join Russia. "Looking ahead, we expect markets to ebb and flow on geopolitical news, but increasingly factor physical indicators," ANZ says. "With northern hemisphere refineries starting to transition to summer fuels in the coming weeks, we expect slightly weaker performance across both benchmarks," the bank says. The U.S. labor market report due later Friday will also provide cues for oil prices. "Payrolls are on tap and markets have been giving every piece of lousy data a free pass to the Bahamas," DBS says. Nymex crude is up 17 cents at $101.73/bbl, Brent crude is up 10 cents at $108.20/bbl. (eric.yep@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0146 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/IDR falls further Friday, implying a stronger rupiah, as the U.S. dollar index got crushed overnight by the sharp rally in EUR/USD triggered by the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  keeping interest rates low. USD/IDR has set a new 4-month low of 11,380 and could now be aiming for the next round-figure barrier at 11,000. The rupiah continues to lead the way for Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , gaining 6.9% this year, helped by persistent inflows from foreign investors fueling demand for high-yielding rupiah-denominated assets. Overall improvement in investor confidence, bolstered by strong U.S. initial jobless claims data released overnight, will further increase risk appetite for high-yielding assets in Indonesia. USD/IDR is now 11,390 versus its Thursday close of 11,465. (ewen.chew@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0137 GMT [Dow Jones] The peso is up as USD/PHP gaps lower at its Friday open, breaking the daily Ichimoku Cloud support at 44.46 and creating a bearish technical bias for the U.S. dollar. A Friday close below the Cloud would also confirm entry into the Bollinger downtrend channel that could open the way for a steeper fall to 44.00 in the near term. The overnight jump in the euro has caused the U.S. dollar index to weaken substantially, triggering a selloff in USD/Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  Friday. The improvement in investor confidence due to upbeat U.S. initial jobless claims data released overnight is also helping risky assets to rally. Inflation USD/PHP is now 44.44 versus its Thursday close of 44.65. (ewen.chew@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0136 GMT [Dow Jones] Australian \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Gov. Glenn Stevens has again made it clear the bank would prefer a lower domestic \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the economy. In his opening statement to the House of Representatives Standing Committee on Economics, the governor merely noted that the Aussie dollar was "still high by historical standards". But in response to questioning, Mr. Stevens added that he retained the view that an AUD/USD rate "in the US$90's or above US$1 is too high". That was enough to push the exchange rate down to US$0.9070 after an earlier rise to US$0.9100. AUD/USD last US$0.9085. (shani.raja@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 March 06, 2014 21:17 ET (02:17 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140307ea370008w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
