\par\par \b \uc2 Asian Morning Briefing: U.S. Stocks End Mostly Up\b0\par\par\uc2 2,171 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2014\par 23:33\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 MARKETS AT A GLANCE\par \par \uc2 (Data as of approximately 5 p.m. ET) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                       LAST    CHANGE  % CHG \par DJIA                 16421.9   61.71   0.38% \par Nasdaq               4352.13   -5.85  -0.13% \par S&P 500              1877.03    3.22   0.17% \par Japan: Nikkei 225    15134.8  237.12   1.59% \par Hang Seng              22703  123.19   0.55% \par Shanghai Composite   2059.58    6.49   0.32% \par S&P BSE Sensex       21513.9  237.01   1.11% \par Australia: S&P/ASX    5445.9    -0.3  -0.01% \par UK: FTSE 100         6788.49   13.07   0.19% \par  \par  \par                     PRICE CHG  YIELD% \par U.S. 2 Year              0/32   0.345 \par U.S. 5 Year             -4/32   1.570 \par U.S. 10 Year            -7/32   2.732 \par Australia 10 Year       -9/32   4.147 \par China 10 Year            5/32   4.530 \par India 10 Year            4/32   8.794 \par Japan 10 Year           -5/32   0.614 \par German 10 Year         -13/32   1.651 \par  \par  \par                           LAST(MID)  CHANGE \par Australia $ (AUD/USD)        0.9086  -0.0003 \par Yen (USD/JPY)                103.14     0.07 \par S. Korean Won (USD/KRW)     1064.39     -0.1 \par Chinese Yuan (USD/CNY)       6.1177  -0.0005 \par Euro (EUR/USD)               1.3862   0.0001 \par WSJ Dollar Index              73.29     0.01 \par  \par  \par                LAST   CHANGE  % CHG \par Crude Oil     101.95     0.5  0.49% \par Brent Crude   107.81    0.52  0.48% \par Gold          1350.6    10.3  0.77% \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SNAPSHOT\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Better-than-expected jobless-claims data helped U.S. stocks finish mostly higher, while pushing Treasurys lower. The euro gained against the dollar and the yen after the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  stood pat on policy and rates. Gold and oil ended up on escalating tensions between Russia and the West.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 OPENING CALL\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Friday is deadline day for Chinese solar-equipment maker Chaori Solar to pay interest on its debt. Market watchers will be paying close attention to whether the government steps in with funds or lets the firm become the first default in China's fast-growing corporate-bond market. Also in focus is February trade data from Taiwan for signs of whether the recovery in the U.S. and Europe starts to show up in the form of better exports from the island, which is a major cog in the electronics production chain.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 EQUITIES\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The S&P 500 index closed at a record high after an upbeat reading on weekly jobless claims, while weakness in shares of drug makers helped push the Nasdaq Composite lower.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Market participants appeared to be selling some strong performers ahead of the government's February employment report due Friday, according to Rick Fier, director of equity trading at Conifer Securities.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The S&P 500 has rallied 5.8% over the past month despite a string of lackluster reports on U.S. manufacturing and housing. Each of the past two monthly jobs reports have missed forecasts, and many have pinned the recent poor economic performance on the cold weather.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Friday's payrolls report is eagerly awaited. Economists expect nonfarm payrolls increased by 152,000 in February, versus 113,000 in January. The unemployment rate is expected to tick down one-tenth of one percent to 6.5%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Joseph Quinlan, chief market strategist for U.S. Trust, said that stocks have been rising in recent weeks because investors anticipate a "strong snapback in growth" after winter ends, particularly from consumers. Stocks are likely to rise should Friday's jobs report come in close to expectations, but investors will likely brush off a disappointing report and attribute any softness to the weather, he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In corporate news, office-supplies retailer Staples slumped 15% after missing fiscal fourth-quarter earnings and revenue estimates. The company said it plans to close 225 stores by the end of 2015 as part of a plan to cut costs by $500 million. Declines also weighed on \f28 \uc2 \f2 Office Depot\f28 \uc2 \f2 , which slid 5.7%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Costco Wholesale\f28 \uc2 \f2  shed 2.8% after reporting fiscal second-quarter earnings and sales that fell short of forecasts. Lower overseas profits and weak results from nonfood merchandise categories weighed on the results.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Pandora Media\f28 \uc2 \f2  declined 5.6% even after reporting an increase last month in listener hours and a boost to its market share.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In Asian trading Thursday, Japan led most stock markets higher, with the Nikkei surging to a five-week high after the government revealed a plan to diversify the portfolio of its giant pension fund.\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 The euro strengthened against major \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , rallying to its highest level against the dollar this year, after the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  decided to leave monetary policy and interest rates unchanged.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The yen struggled, particularly against the euro and the dollar, following upbeat jobless claims data in the U.S. and the results of the ECB meeting. Both drove the value of the Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  lower.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar advanced against the yen as the U.S. labor market showed progress. The Labor Department reported that initial claims for jobless benefits fell 26,000 to a seasonally adjusted 323,000 during the week ended March 1, the lowest level since November.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The number came in below forecasts of 335,000 and showed a job market improving despite persistent severe weather. The number arrived before Friday's jobs number, which the Federal Reserve watches closely to determine to what degree the economy is recovering.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Fed has linked its bond-buying program to the health of the U.S. economy. As it has shown signs of improvement, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  decided to make measured cuts to its bond purchases. The Fed's program has weighed on the dollar, so the cuts in bond purchases have given the greenback a boost.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 There is a broad expectation that U.S. data should improve as the weather warms and the drag and uncertainty surrounding the "weather effect" on the numbers dissipate, according to Richard Cochinos, head of Americas G-10 \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategy at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Citigroup\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "For now, better U.S. data means higher risk appetite--and potentially underperformance of the dollar [against] the New Zealand dollar and the euro," Mr. Cochinos wrote in a research report. "A broadly stronger dollar would come later in the month when U.S. data accelerates and the strength of U.S. outperformance becomes clear."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 BONDS\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Treasury bonds dropped after better-than-expected data on weekly jobless claims buoyed sentiment and weakened demand for haven government debt.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Treasury bonds took an additional hit as selling of \f28 \uc2 \f2 German government\f28 \uc2 \f2  bonds, another major safe-haven market, spilled over, traders said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The selling was driven by comments from European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  President Mario Draghi, who suggested the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  would take its time before adding more stimulus amid signs of improvement in the euro zone's economy. Mr. Draghi disappointed some investors who expected him to signal further easing measures as soon as April.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Labor Department reported that the number of Americans filing new claims for unemployment benefits fell last week by a bigger-than-forecast 26,000 to a seasonally adjusted 323,000 in the week ended March 1, the lowest level since November.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The data come ahead of Friday's nonfarm-payrolls report. Economists expect the economy to have added 152,000 jobs last month, more than the 113,000 added in January. The unemployment rate is seen falling to 6.5% from 6.6% a month ago. The monthly nonfarm-payrolls release is one of the main reports influencing the Federal Reserve's interest-rate policy outlook.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "A number that is higher than expected could do some real damage to the Treasury market," said Alex Manzara, vice president of rate futures at brokerage \f28 \uc2 \f2 R.J. O'Brien & Associates\f28 \uc2 \f2  in Chicago.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 COMMODITIES\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Gold prices rose while palladium set a fresh 11-month high after tensions between Russia and the West escalated.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Gold prices have been steadily climbing in recent weeks, as a series of geopolitical flare-ups stoked investor demand for wealth-preserving assets. Some traders view gold as a haven because it is a widely accepted reserve asset without links to a particular government or country.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. President Barrack Obama said that Crimea's referendum on seceding from Ukraine to join Russia is illegal and added that the U.S. and \f28 \uc2 \f2 European Union\f28 \uc2 \f2  are united against Russia's "\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 " in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ukraine\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Russian troops entered \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ukraine\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's Crimea region late last week, escalating the conflict between the former Soviet state and the West.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Gold's catching a bid any time there's instability," said Adam Klopfenstein, a senior market strategist with Archer Financial Services Inc. "People are going to want to gravitate toward safe-havens like gold anytime questions about the Ukrainian situation re-emerge," he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Palladium futures rocketed to their highest level since April 1 after the U.S. paved the way for sanctions against Russia.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Crude-oil futures rose for the first time in three days as concerns over the political situation in Crimea were rekindled.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TODAY'S HEADLINES\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. Jobless Claims Show Labor Market Improvement\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The number of Americans filing new claims for unemployment benefits fell last week to the lowest level since November, a sign of improvement for a labor market grappling with unusually cold and stormy weather.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Crimea to Hold Referendum on Joining Russia\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Moscow-backed government of Crimea set a referendum in 10 days to ratify its decision to secede from Ukraine and join Russia, dramatically escalating tension as the West tries to negotiate a withdrawal of Russian troops from the area.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ECB Leaves Rates Unchanged\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ECB President Mario Draghi reiterated that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is ready to act if needed, but refrained from introducing new stimulus measures to help the euro-zone recovery. The ECB kept interest rates unchanged.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Cerberus Reaches Preliminary $9 Billion Deal for Safeway\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Cerberus Capital Management\f28 \uc2 \f2  has reached a preliminary agreement to buy Safeway for more than $9 billion, according to people familiar with the matter, in what would be the private-equity firm's latest acquisition of a big U.S. grocery chain.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 6 Mar 2014 17:33 EDT \par Asian Morning Briefing: U.S. Stocks End Mostly Up -2-\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Chaori Solar Not Expecting Bailout\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A Chinese solar company facing the country's first bond default said that, for now, it wouldn't receive a local-government bailout.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Wells Fargo\f28 \uc2 \f2 , \f28 \uc2 \f2 HSBC\f28 \uc2 \f2  Agree to Refunds\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Wells Fargo\f28 \uc2 \f2  and \f28 \uc2 \f2 HSBC\f28 \uc2 \f2  have agreed to pay refunds to potentially hundreds of thousands of borrowers under deals to end allegations the banks overcharged for homeowners insurance, court filings show.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Fed's Plosser Calls on Central Banks to Get Back to Basics\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Central banks should give up their efforts to manipulate financial markets and return their focus to delivering durable economic growth and stable prices, Federal Reserve Bank of Philadelphia President Charles Plosser said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Judge Denies Apple Request to Ban Samsung Products\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A U.S. judge denied Apple's request to seek a permanent injunction against \f28 \uc2 \f2 Samsung Electronics\f28 \uc2 \f2 ' products, dealing a setback in the company's legal fight over patents used in mobile products.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Workers Strike \f28 \uc2 \f2 IBM\f28 \uc2 \f2  Plant in China\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Workers struck an \f28 \uc2 \f2 IBM\f28 \uc2 \f2  plant in China as the company prepares to transfer the computer-server factory to Lenovo.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 RECENT DJ DOMINANTS\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 JPM's Masters Likely to Stay With Bank Until Commodities Unit Sale Complete\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Cerberus Reaches Preliminary $9 Billion Deal for Safeway\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Annapolis Capital Raises C$200 Million for New Energy-Focused Private-Equity Fund\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Insys Holder Sells Shares After Massive Post-IPO Rally\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Troubles Continue for Italy's Monte dei Paschi Ahead of Earnings Release\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TODAY'S CALENDAR\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Times in GMT, followed by country and event)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2145 NZ Q4 Wholesale Trade Survey\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2230 AUS Feb Australian PCI\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2230 AUS \f28 \uc2 \f2 Reserve Bank of Australia\f28 \uc2 \f2  Governor appears before Economics Committee\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2350 JPN Feb Provisional Trade Statistics for 1st 20 days of Month\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2350 JPN Feb International Reserves / Foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 0001 UK Feb REC & KPMG Report on Jobs\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0500 JPN Jan Indexes of Business Conditions - Preliminary Release\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0530 AUS Feb Official Reserve Assets\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0700 GER Jan WPI\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0730 THA Weekly International Reserves\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0745 FRA Jan Foreign trade\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0800 TAI Feb Merchandise trade\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0815 HK Feb \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Foreign Exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Reserves\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0830 HK Q4 Quarterly Statistics on Vessels, Port Cargo & Containers\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0900 SIN Feb Official Foreign Reserves\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0900 MAL Feb International Reserves, end of month\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0900 ITA Jan PPI\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0930 UK Q4 Bank of England external business stats\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0930 UK Feb Bank of England / GfK NOP Quarterly Inflation Attitudes Survey\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1000 EU Q4 Balance of payments - 1st release\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1001 MAL Jan External Trade\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1100 GER Jan Industrial Production Index\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1130 IND Weekly \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1330 US Jan U.S. International Trade in Goods & Services\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1330 US Feb U.S. Employment Report\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1330 CAN Jan International merchandise trade\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1330 CAN Q4 Labour productivity, hourly compensation & unit labour cost\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1330 CAN Feb Labour Force Survey\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1700 US FRB New York President William Dudley speech at Brooklyn College\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1900 CAN \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Canada\f28 \uc2 \f2  Weekly Financial Statistics\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2000 US Jan Consumer Credit\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 March 06, 2014 17:33 ET (22:33 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140306ea36004gh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
