\par\par \b \uc2 CEE MARKETS 3-Assets firmer but some lose ground after Crimea vote\b0\par\par\uc2 962 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2014\par 17:36\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2014 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (Adds afternoon retreat, fresh Hungarian bond sale figure)\par     * Hungary sells more bonds than planned, yields fall\par     * Tension over Ukraine, Romanian domestic politics eased\par     * Crimean vote trims CEE asset gains, hits shares\par     * Serbian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  keeps rates on hold\par \par     By Sandor Peto\par     BUDAPEST, March 6 (Reuters) - Central European markets\par mostly firmed up on Thursday as diplomatic efforts to cool the\par crisis in Ukraine supported riskier assets, and Hungary sold\par more bonds than planned at an auction. \par     Most regional \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and stocks continued to recover\par from jitters caused by the situation in neighbouring Ukraine,\par the second hit to the region this year from developments in\par other emerging economies.\par     "Near-term price action remains largely a function of the\par uncertain geopolitical outlook," Raiffeisen said in a note,\par adding that sentiment was also improved by Wednesday's data\par confirming an economic pick-up in Hungary and Romania.\par     \f28 \uc2 \f2 European Union\f28 \uc2 \f2  leaders were set to warn but not sanction\par Russia on Thursday over its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ukraine\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , after\par Moscow rebuffed a diplomatic push for it to pull its forces in\par Crimea back to their bases. \par     With fear of a war to the east receding, Hungary's bond\par auctions attracted healthy demand. It sold 85.4 billion forints\par worth of three-, five- and 10-year bonds - 50 percent more than\par planned.\par     Hungary's three-year bond yields were down 6 basis points\par from Wednesday at 4.7 percent. The bonds were sold at 4.66\par percent at the auctions, their lowest level since January.\par Long-term bonds gave up their gains, however.\par     Traders said investors were scrambling to cover short\par positions built in recent weeks, betting for yield rises.\par     "Demand was particularly high for short-end bonds," one\par Budapest-based trader said. "Some people may buy based on\par increasingly dovish \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  comments, like in Poland, and\par the European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may also loosen conditions somehow."\par     Polish bonds firmed slightly. They surged on Wednesday after\par the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  cut inflation forecasts and said it would keep\par rates flat at least until the fourth quarter of this year.\par  \par     The zloty and the forint dipped against\par the euro after Crimea's parliament voted to join Russia. But by\par late trade they had gained 0.3 percent, helped by the euro's\par  surge as the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  signalled no need for\par new economic stimulus.\par     The region's equities fell after the Crimean news, however.\par Shares in OTP Bank and drug maker Richter -\par Hungarian firms that have business in Russia and Ukraine - fell\par 2 and 2.4 percent, respectively, dragging Budapest's main index\par  almost 1 percent lower. The main indices in other\par regional capitals rose 0.4-0.7 percent.    \par \par     ROMANIAN POLITICS HELP NOW\par     Romanian assets got local backing from easing domestic\par political tensions on Wednesday.\par     President Traian Basescu ratified a review of a 4 billion \par euro aid deal with the IMF, ending a weeks-long standoff, and\par endorsed a newly formed coalition government just days after he\par threatened to block its creation. \par     The leu was flat, but Romania's five-year bond\par yield dropped to 4.9 percent from 4.97 percent in the previous\par session, and 5.05 percent three days ago.\par     The government sold 700 million lei worth of Treasury bonds\par due in June 2017, as planned. Last month Romania\par failed to sell debt at three tenders as the political standoff\par and escalating violence in Ukraine weighed on assets.\par     Serbia's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  kept its benchmark rate on\par hold at 9.5 percent for a third month running on Thursday, as\par expected, amid uncertainty over a mid-March election, pressure\par on the dinar and a pick-up in inflation.\par     The dinar firmed up a shade. The bank has been\par buying the dinar in the market for weeks to protect it from\par contagion from abroad and pre-election uncertainty over fiscal\par policies.\par \par                       CEE MARKETS SNAPSHOT AT 1634 CET\par  ************************** \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  ************************\par                              Latest  Previous   Daily   Change\par                              bid     close      change  in 2014\par  Czech crown                 27.327    27.355  +0.10% +0.02%\par  Hungarian forint           308.550   309.400  +0.28%  -3.75%\par  Polish zloty                 4.173     4.186   +0.33%  -0.55%\par  Romanian leu                 4.497     4.496   -0.02%   -0.82%\par  Croatian kuna                7.653     7.654   +0.01%  -0.46%\par  Serbian dinar              115.900   115.960   +0.05%   -1.21%\par  Note: daily change calculated from previous close at 1700 GMT\par  **************************** STOCKS **************************\par                              Latest  Previous   Daily   Change\par                                      close      change  in 2014\p