\par\par \b \uc2 CORRECTED-Rouble weakens as Ukraine concerns weigh\b0\par\par\uc2 379 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2014\par 09:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2014 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Corrects figures in paragraph 4 on scale of Tuesday's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , changes headline and lead)\par \par \uc2 MOSCOW, March 6 (Reuters) - The rouble opened down against the dollar on Thursday as concerns about the Ukraine crisis continued to weigh.\par \par \uc2 Moscow stock indexes saw fragile gains, tracking a reprieve in Asian markets after diplomatic efforts seemed to slightly moderate the crisis over Russia's military \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 At 0635 GMT, the rouble was down 0.3 percent against the dollar at 36.15 and down 0.2 percent versus the broadly weaker euro at 49.63, indicating that investors nonetheless remained worried about the possibility of Russia launching an overt invasion of Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Data showed on Thursday that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  spent just over $300 million of its gold and \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves on Tuesday, despite the rouble's firming that day, after spending $11.3 billion on Monday to keep the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from falling too fast.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  releases its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  data with a two-day delay.\par \par \uc2 Analysts at VTB Capital reckon that the rouble is about 8.0-8.5 percent behind the emerging markets average index so far this year, and that Ukraine's political crisis has accounted for 2.0-3.0 percentage points of that.\par \par \uc2 "Now that is fading away, but we are not quite sure that the premium for political risks will disappear completely in the near term," the analysts wrote in a morning note.\par \par \uc2 "Also, we think the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may cut the volume of accumulated \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the near future as the market calms down."\par \par \uc2 The dollar-denominated RTS index was up 0.2 percent to 1,184.2 points and the rouble-traded MICEX gained 0.6 percent to 1,359.2 points.\par \par \uc2 "Russian financial markets remain vulnerable, as investors in Russian and regional assets are tuned primarily to news flow from the Crimea and Ukraine, with no breakthroughs so far," Uralsib analysts wrote in a morning note.\par \par \uc2 For rouble poll data see\par \par \uc2 For Russian equities guide see\par \par \uc2 For Russian treasury bonds see\par \par \uc2 Russia in graphics: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://link.reuters.com/dun63s"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://link.reuters.com/dun63s}}}\uc2  ($1 = 36.0817 Russian roubles) (Reporting by Lidia Kelly and Vladimir Abramov; Editing by Mark Trevelyan)\par \par \uc2 Released: 2014-3-9T08:00:14.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020140306ea36007c3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
