\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Poland says to leave rates flat until end of third quarter\b0\par\par\uc2 619 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2014\par 17:38\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2014 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  extends rates guidance by three months\par \par \uc2 * Move was partly to reassure markets rattled by Ukraine\par \par \uc2 * Governor says don't assume rates will rise in Q4\par \par \uc2 * Says Ukraine crisis showed benefits of euro membership   (Adds quotes, market reaction)\par \par \uc2 By Karolina Slowikowska and Christian Lowe\par \par \uc2 March 5 (Reuters) - Poland said on Wednesday it would keep interest rates flat at least until the fourth quarter of this year, three months longer than its previous guidance, and said the need to reassure markets rattled by the Ukraine crisis influenced its decision.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Marek Belka said that fresh projections showed the recovery in Poland, eastern Europe's biggest economy, was robust and that inflation was low, so there was no pressure to change rates from their 2.50 percent level.\par \par \uc2 But he said Russia's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Crimea Peninsula -- which unleashed a wave of market jitters around eastern Europe, including in Poland -- had persuaded the bank's rate-setters to act sooner than it might otherwise have done to extend the forward guidance on rates.\par \par \uc2 That decision surprised the market, and yields on Polish 10-year bonds fell by 17 basis points to their lowest point in three months. The zloty was unchanged.\par \par \uc2 "The situation in Ukraine sped up our decision to show that we do not see risks for the Polish economy, at least in the sphere of monetary policy," Belka told a news conference.\par \par \uc2 "We wanted to express our confidence in the fundamentals of the Polish economy, and thus in the stability of the zloty."\par \par \uc2 The bank earlier had left rates unchanged, as expected, and released new projections for inflation and gross domestic product (GDP) growth. It had said this month's projections would be decisive in determining its stance on rates.\par \par \uc2 The new forecasts put GDP growth for this year at between 2.9 and 4.2 percent, up from the previous projection of between 2.0-3.9 percent. Inflation was projected to be in the 0.8-1.4 percent range this year, lower than previously forecast.\par \par \uc2 RATE HIKES NOT A DONE DEAL\par \par \uc2 Many in the market anticipate that the next rate move once the forward guidance expires will be an increase, to ensure the improving economy does not overheat. Analysts polled by Reuters expected the hikes would come in the fourth quarter\par \par \uc2 But Belka cautioned against assuming that was a done deal, saying rates could stay flat for longer.\par \par \uc2 "One should not expect that as soon as the third quarter ends, the (rate-setting) Council will take up its pick axes and spades and get down to work," he said.\par \par \uc2 Belka said he did not expect any major lasting impact on the Polish economy from the events in Ukraine. Both Ukraine and Russia are major trading partners for Poland, leaving Warsaw vulnerable if sanctions are imposed.\par \par \uc2 Belka said Ukraine and its repercussions could in theory knock a fraction of one percent off GDP growth, but the economy was sufficiently robust that the effects would not be more pronounced.\par \par \uc2 If exports to Russia and Ukraine drop, causing a surplus of goods in the domestic market, that could have the effect of slowing inflation.\par \par \uc2 In another echo from the crisis in Ukraine, Poland's neighbour to the east, Belka said events there had made him more positive on the benefits of Poland joining the euro single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Belka, who has previously said he backs Polish accession but at a cautious pace, said political instability underlined the value of being in a big economic bloc.   (Additional reporting by Marcin Goettig, Marcin Goclowski and Michal Janusz)\par \par \uc2 Released: 2014-3-8T16:38:02.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020140305ea3500flb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
