\par\par \b \uc2 Should Investors Start Loving Russia? -- Money Talks\b0\par\par\uc2 By Alen Mattich   \par \uc2 577 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2014\par 05:20\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires Korean (English)\par \uc2 RTNWK\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 The ruble and Russian equities had taken a considerable beating well before Ukraine's political crisis erupted. But the selling gathered pace when the shooting in Kiev started. And then, when it seemed that Russia was invading Crimea, Ukraine's militarily sensitive and ethnically Russia southern province, the markets plunged. Russia's RTS dollar-denominated equity index nosedived 12% in a day.\par \par \uc2 Just under three years ago, the index was trading at a little more than 2100. By the time it bottomed on Monday, the RTS was only just over 1100--around a 48% tumble. Of course, emerging market equities have generally taken a beating lately. Indeed, Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  blamed equity market weakness on the U.S. Federal Reserve's decision to trim its bond-buying program. Even so, Russia has seriously underperformed other markets. The MSCI emerging markets index is down 9.7% over the past year. The MSCI Russia index is down 23.9% during the same period.\par \par \uc2 The ruble has routinely hit new lows. In the spring of 2011, a dollar was worth 28 rubles. By the start of this year it was 33. At one point on Monday, it was nearly 37.\par \par \uc2 For a time, Russia's monetary authorities treated the ruble with benign neglect. Its depreciation was seen as broadly helpful in making the country more competitive at a time when economic growth had started to flag. But the recent declines had become so precipitous that on Monday, the \uc2 Russian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  raised its key interest rate by 1.5 percentage points to 7% in order to defend the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The negative stories are still there. Ukraine's internal politics haven't been resolved and Russia's involvement complicates things. Civil war is still a possibility. Russian military \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine\b0 \highlight\uc2 's ethnic-Russian regions could trigger sanctions from the West. That's not to mention Russia's own long-term problems of a sclerotic economy, corruption, lack of corporate transparency and increasingly autocratic politics.\par \par \uc2 But in the near term, adventurous investors might well find attractive propositions.\par \par \uc2 "At these prices, you don't have to take a very optimistic view of Russia's future to see some potential bargains," according to Stephanie Flanders, \uc2 J.P. Morgan Asset Management\uc2 's chief European market strategist.\par \par \uc2 Oil prices have rallied sharply since the start of the year. Given that Russia is a major producer, higher prices should boost government finances and shore up the country's current account surplus. This also makes the ruble less of a losing proposition. With lower \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk, Russia's interest rate spread becomes attractive again, which should draw in capital.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, valuation multiples look attractive--with equities trading at 3.9 times earnings and 0.6 times book value, they've only been cheaper at the worst points of the 2008 financial crisis, according to \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Some of that bargain hunting seemed to kick in on Tuesday, with both Russia's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and equities bouncing sharply. By early afternoon, the RTS index was up more than 6% on the day.\par \par \uc2 This is an opinion column by Alen Mattich, who has been a columnist for \uc2 Dow Jones\uc2  for more than a decade. Write to him at alen.mattich@dowjones.com or on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  @AlenMattich\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 March 04, 2014 08:36 ET (13:36 GMT)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 05-03-14 0420GMT\par \par \uc2 Dokument RTNWK00020140305ea350000d\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 Dukascopy Bank SA Research Division: Yen drops most in two weeks since Ukraine risks ease\b0\par\par\uc2 527 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2014\par \uc2 News Bites - Private Companies\par \uc2 NBPRCO\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2014. News Bites Pty Ltd.   \par \par \uc2 NEWS BITES - PRIVATE COMPANIES\par \par \uc2 [Company Release]\par \par \uc2 The Japanese Yen fell to the lowest point in two weeks against the Dollar since president of Russia, Vladimir Putin, stated that no direct necessity is needed to send forces to Ukraine, thus curbing safe asset demand. The Yen declined against all of the 16 major peers, while the Ruble gained. Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  depreciated 0.4% to 101.85 against the Dollar at 6:51 a.m. in NY, sliding to the lowest decline since February 18 and dropped 0.6% to 140.19 versus the Euro, which allowed the common \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to strengthen 0.2% to $1.3764.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.dukascopy.com/swiss/english/marketwatch/market_news/Market-News-and-Research/41182/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Click here for the full original announcement}}}\par \par \uc2 BUSINESS NEWS ROUND UP\par \par \uc2 04 March 2014: Dukascopy Bank SA Research Division: Brent falls after rising yesterday on Ukraine's easing tension\par \par \uc2 [Company Release] The European benchmark Brent crude slipped on Tuesday session after rising notable yesterday as the Russian Federation entered Ukraine's Crimea peninsula, however war conflict is unlikely to happen according to experts. Brent for delivery in April fell 1.9%, or $2.09, to $109.11 per barrel on the London's ICE Futures Europe exchange. Source: Company Website\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.dukascopy.com/swiss/english/marketwatch/market_news/Market-News-and-Research/41192/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Click here for the full original announcement}}}\par \par \uc2 04 March 2014: Dukascopy Bank SA Research Division: WTI slips as Russia-Ukraine tension eases\par \par \uc2 [Company Release] West Texas Intermediate oil declined on Tuesday trading session falling from the strongest level since September on speculation that the escalating geopolitical tension between the Russian Federation and Ukraine is unlikely to end up in a war conflict. WTI for settlement in April slipped $1.62 to $103.30 per barrel on the NYMEX and was last seen at $103.69 by 1:29 p.m.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.dukascopy.com/swiss/english/marketwatch/market_news/Market-News-and-Research/41191/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Click here for the full original announcement}}}\par \par \uc2 03 March 2014: Dukascopy Bank SA Research Division: \b \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Hryvnia falls to record low as Russian \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 [Company Release] The \b \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Hryvnia declined on Monday trading session falling towards the lowest level all-time after the Russian Federation \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  into \b \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Crimea peninsula raising concerns over possible war conflict as the Russian President Putin declares his troops had right to enter the country.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.dukascopy.com/swiss/english/marketwatch/market_news/Market-News-and-Research/41103/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Click here for the full original announcement}}}\par \par \uc2 03 March 2014: Dukascopy Bank SA Research Division: U.S. shares fall on Russian \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 [Company Release] Wall Street edged lower on Monday trading session together with most risk assets world-wide after Russian \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened Ukraine\b0 \highlight\uc2  raising concerns over possible war conflict as the President of Russian Federation Vladimir Putin claimed the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was correct.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.dukascopy.com/swiss/english/marketwatch/market_news/Market-News-and-Research/41100/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Click here for the full original announcement}}}\par \par \uc2 03 March 2014: Dukascopy Bank SA Research Division: Bank of Russia raises rates by 150 basis points\par \par \uc2 [Company Release] The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Russian increased its benchmark interest rates unexpectedly on Monday amid raising concerns over possible war conflict in neighboring Ukraine pushing the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Ruble towards the historical low versus the U.S. Dollar. The Bank of Russia raised the rate by 150 basis points from 5.50% to 7.00%. Source: Company Website\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.dukascopy.com/swiss/english/marketwatch/market_news/Market-News-and-Research/41104/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Click here for the full original announcement}}}\par \par \uc2 Dokument NBPRCO0020140305ea35001a2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
