\par\par \b \uc2 Russian Markets Shudder Under Threat Of Sanctions\b0\par\par\uc2 1,078 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2014\par 01:47\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par A1\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    (FROM THE WALL STREET JOURNAL 3/4/14) \par    By Greg White, Laurence Norman and Andrey Ostroukh \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 MOSCOW -- A Russian weakness was exposed in its confrontation with the West over Ukraine, as its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  hit a record low and its stock market plunged in the face of U.S. and European warnings of sanctions.\f28 \par \par \uc2 President Barack Obama said the U.S. is looking at economic and diplomatic measures to isolate Moscow, which he said is "on the wrong side of history" as well as international law with its occupation of the Crimea region of Ukraine. Further military moves into the country, he warned, "will be a costly proposition for Russia."\par \par \uc2 The Kremlin has dismissed any sanctions as inappropriate and showed no sign of backing down under the pressure, which so far has been primarily in the form of tough talk.\par \par \uc2 European foreign ministers backed off any immediate action on sanctions Monday, despite what some called the gravest threat to European stability since the end of the Cold War.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 U.S. State Department\uc2  said that punitive measures are likely if Moscow does not temper its military \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but wouldn't give details. Spokeswoman Jennifer Psaki indicated they would go beyond things like visa freezes to measures with economic bite.\par \par \uc2 The developments highlighted the West's limited options for deterring a Russia determined to reassert its influence in a neighbor with which it shares centuries of common history and culture as well as myriad economic ties.\par \par \uc2 Russian troops occupying the Crimean peninsula in southern Ukraine tightened their control, raising pressure on the few Ukrainian military units based there, but stopping short of violence. The Kremlin drew up plans for urgent financial aid to its new clients in the predominantly Russia-speaking region, and even ordered preparations for building a massive bridge to link it directly to Russia.\par \par \uc2 Ukraine's prime minister admitted that his government has little near-term hope of regaining control of the region.\par \par \uc2 President Vladimir Putin on Monday visited massive military maneuvers under way outside St. Petersburg, arriving in a helicopter to view tanks and paratroopers in live-fire exercises.\par \par \uc2 The Russian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sharply raised interest rates Monday to help stem the ruble's slide, which could push Russia's already sputtering economy into recession if the hike lasts for long.\par \par \uc2 But the impact would be eased thanks to high oil prices -- they rose Monday on the turmoil -- which would mean the Kremlin's coffers would likely remain flush with rubles needed to shore up public support. Russia is one of the world's biggest energy exporters.\par \par \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Mario Draghi said the direct impact of the Ukraine crisis on the continent's economy would be relatively limited. Still, he added, "the geopolitical dimensions" of the crisis could have effects that "go beyond the actual links."\par \par \uc2 Some tremors were already being felt: U.S. wheat futures surged 4.6% as traders fretted that the escalating crisis will slow grain exports from Ukraine, a major producer.\par \par \uc2 Rising tensions are clouding sales forecasts and investment plans for global corporate giants such as \uc2 Renault SA\uc2  and \uc2 PepsiCo Inc\uc2 . that are exposed to Russia's market.\par \par \uc2 Shares in European banks with \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Russia or Ukraine, including Italy's \uc2 UniCredit SpA\uc2  and France's \uc2 Societe Generale SA\uc2 , got clobbered as markets across Europe were rattled. The Stoxx Europe 600 index fell 2.3%, and countries with closer economic ties to Russia suffered more. Germany's benchmark DAX dropped 3.4%. Poland's main stock index fell 5.2%.\par \par \uc2 In Brussels, European foreign ministers condemned what the \uc2 European Union\uc2  foreign policy chief Catherine Ashton called Russia's "act of aggression" in Crimea.\par \par \uc2 The ministers said that "in the absence of de-escalating steps by Russia," the EU would consider suspending talks with Russia on easing visa procedures and on a trade and economic agreement.\par \par \uc2 The warning was an attempt to strike a balance, refraining from hasty action that would foreclose Russia's ability to save face, while simultaneously signaling that the EU wouldn't hold off for long.\par \par \uc2 So far, the pressure on Moscow has been limited mainly to stern rhetoric and canceled meetings, including preparations for the \uc2 Group of Eight\uc2  summit in Sochi, Russia, in June. The White House said it won't send a presidential delegation to the Paralympic Games in Sochi either.\par \par \uc2 In Europe, at least, there appears to be little appetite for broader sanctions that would upend trade with Russia.\par \par \uc2 France, for instance, is on schedule to deliver the first of two Mistral carriers, valued at 1.4 billion euros ($1.9 billion), to the Russian Navy by the end of the year, according to \uc2 DCNS\uc2 , the French firm building the high-tech amphibious assault ships.\par \par \uc2 Asked Monday whether Moscow's incursion was forcing Paris to rethink its trade ties, French Foreign Minister Laurent Fabius said: "We're not there. For the moment, we are trying to stop Russia's mobilization in Ukraine and establish a dialogue."\par \par \uc2 Diplomats said that Germany argued strongly in the meeting for Europe to move cautiously and to ensure that channels of communication were kept open to the Kremlin.\par \par \uc2 Along with Russian stocks, which fell about 10%, the shares of Western companies like Renault, Danish brewer \uc2 Carlsberg A/S\uc2  and British oil giant \uc2 BP PLC\uc2  also fell on fears their big Russian investments would suffer as tensions over Ukraine grow.\par \par \uc2 Russia is \uc2 PepsiCo\uc2 's second-largest market by revenue after the U.S. The Purchase, N.Y.-based snack and beverage giant has billions of dollars of assets in the country. It declined to comment on Monday.\par \par \uc2 Entanglements between Russia and Europe go much further than with U.S. companies. Europe is a major buyer of Russian natural gas; Russia is a strong market for German goods; energy companies in Italy, the U.K. and elsewhere have major investments in Russia.\par \par \uc2 The ties make punishing Russia practically difficult because of the divisions within Europe between those close to Russia and those with less to lose.\par \par \uc2 But \uc2 Charles Grant\uc2 , director of the \uc2 Centre for European Reform\uc2 , a London-based think tank, warned against exaggerating Russia's economic leverage over Europe.\par \par \uc2 Cutting off gas supplies to Europe via Ukraine -- a perennial Russian threat -- would do plenty of damage to Russia too, he said. "We need (Russia), and it needs us."\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 March 03, 2014 19:47 ET (00:47 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140304ea3400070\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
