\par\par \b \uc2 CEE MARKETS 2-Assets rebound, Ukraine crisis still a threat\b0\par\par\uc2 1,087 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2014\par 18:14\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2014 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (Adds bonds, updates markets)\par     * Markets bounce back as Ukraine/Russia tension eases\par     * Return of wobbles remains a threat, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ukraine\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is main factor\par watched\par     * Romania, Serbia market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  have not eroded\par reserves\par \par     By Sandor Peto\par     BUDAPEST, March 4 (Reuters) - Central European assets\par rebounded on Tuesday after President \f28 \uc2 \f2 Vladimir Putin\f28 \uc2 \f2  said Russia\par saw no need to use military force in Ukraine for now. \par     Putin's comments, and an earlier order for Russian troops\par involved in a military exercise near the border with Ukraine to\par return to base, were seen as an attempt to ease East-West\par tension and fears of war in the former Soviet republic.\par     That helped lift stocks and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European\par Union\f28 \uc2 \f2 's emerging east, which had tumbled as Russian assets were\par hit by panic-selling on Monday.\par     Shares in Hungary's OTP bank, which has some 140\par branches in Ukraine, led gains, firming 5.8 percent after the\par previous day's plunge of 10 percent to 18-month lows.\par     But the situation in Ukraine is still "a time bomb" for\par central and east European markets, one Budapest-based \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par dealer said, with investors eyeing the risk that Kiev will\par default on debt as well as the possibility of military conflict.\par  \par     "Clearly the Ukrainian issue moves everything in the region\par now," the dealer added.    \par     Moscow's forces remain in control of Crimea, seized\par bloodlessly after Russia's ally Viktor Yanukovich was ousted as\par Ukrainian president last month. In his first comments on the\par crisis, Putin said all options could be used to protect\par compatriots who were living in "terror" in Ukraine.\par  \par     The turmoil in Ukraine has been a fresh blow to emerging\par markets, many of which have already had to hike interest rates\par and defend their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  this year as investors fret about\par U.S. stimulus withdrawal and Chinese economic growth. \par     Exports from the EU's eastern states to Ukraine are around\par only 1-2 percent of their foreign sales, but a debt default or\par war could have an impact in the region's markets.\par     Central European states' economic fundamentals are\par relatively strong but investors have tended to sell emerging\par market assets indiscriminately this year. \par     Trading on Tuesday was thin and the tone still jittery but\par Hungary's forint and Poland's zloty regained\par some of the ground lost the day before, firming 0.7 and 0.3\par percent against the euro, respectively.\par     One Budapest-based trader said local banks had bought\par Hungarian government debt, mainly long-term paper yielding more\par than 5 percent - a big pick up from the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's 2.7\par percent two-week deposit rate.\par     "Foreigners bought the papers, too, and there was a rebound\par in Poland," the trader said. "We will see now if there are\par sanctions against Russia and what their response will be. In my\par view the Ukrainian story is not over yet."\par     \par     RISK CHANNELS\par     The forint is seen as vulnerable to contagion from other\par emerging markets because Hungary is the region's most indebted\par state and its interest rates are low following a long series of\par cuts. The country is also due to hold elections in April.\par     Firms with business interests in Ukraine, such as Hungarian\par drug maker Richter and Poland's PKO \f28 \uc2 \f2 BP\f28 \uc2 \f2 , could\par also be hurt by more financial turbulence or war there.\par     Some investors also believe Romanian rate cuts have been too\par deep given contagion risks. The country's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has had\par to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to prop up the leu following a split in\par the government last month, before which the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of the EU's\par second poorest member had been stable for several months.\par     Romanian Prime Minister Victor Ponta won a confidence vote\par in parliament late on Tuesday, which gave him a new mandate to\par push through IMF-backed reforms. \par     Prior to the vote, the leu had gained 0.2 percent\par in illiquid late trade, after earlier failing to benefit from\par the improved regional mood.\par     Serbia's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has also stepped up \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to\par defend the dinar as the country prepares for early elections on\par March 16, though as with Romania its foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves\par remain healthy.\par     The bank is expected to keep rates on hold on Thursday given\par uncertainty pending a mid-March election, pressure on the dinar\par and a pickup in inflation. \par     The dinar firmed 0.1 percent against the euro on\par Tuesday. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  had spent 50 million euros in the\par market on Monday to defend the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , the same amount raised\par by the Serbian debt agency in a Treasury bill auction.\par     \f28 \uc2 \f2 Poland's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is also expected to leave its\par benchmark interest rate unchanged at its monthly meeting on\par Wednesday and to start raising late this year. \par     \par                       CEE MARKETS SNAPSHOT AT 1647 CET\par  ************************** \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  ************************\par                              Latest  Previous   Daily   Change\par                              bid     close      change  in 2014\par  Czech crown                 27.351    27.346   -0.02%  -0.07%\par  Hungarian forint           310.770   312.900  +0.69%  -4.44%\par  Polish zloty                 4.188     4.200   +0.28%  -0.91%\par  Romanian leu                 4.507     4.515  +0.18%   -1.04%\par  Croatian kuna                7.652     7.652    0.00%  -0.44%\par  Serbian dinar              115.800   115.940   +0.12%   -1.12%\par  Note: daily change calculated from previous close at 1700 GMT\par  **************************** STOCKS **************************\par                              Latest  Previous   Daily   Change\par                                      close      change  in 2014\par  Prague                      1001.70    991.44  +1.03% +1.50%\par  Budapest                  17671.28  17115.16   +3.25%   -3.62%\par  Warsaw                     2448.05   2389.89   +2.43% +1.82%\par  Bucharest                  6295.94   6190.96  +1.70%   -2.81%\par  ***************************** BONDS **************************\par                          Yield    Yield    Spread    Daily\par                          (bid)    change   vs Bund   change in\par  Czech Republic                                      spread\par    2-year                0.745    0.000   +63bps    +1bps\par    5-year                1.130     -0.036   +51bps      -5bps\par   10-year                2.214   +0.022   +62bps     -1bps\par  Hungary\par    3-year                4