\par\par \b \uc2 Grand Central: Evans vs. Taylor -- War of the Fed Policy Rules -- WSJ Blog\b0\par\par\uc2 1,861 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2014\par 17:42\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal's Daily Report on Global Central Banks for Tuesday, March 4\par \par \uc2 Sign up for the newsletter: on.wsj.com/grandcentralsignup .\par \par \uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Highlights \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    -- Da Costa's Take: Evans vs. Taylor - War of the Fed Policy Rules \par  \par  \par    -- ECB Watching Ukraine Crisis Closely: Draghi \par  \par  \par    -- Crimea Conflict Makes Poland Rethink Euro, Central Banker Says \par  \par  \par    -- China \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Guides Short-Term Rates Higher \par  \par  \par    -- BOJ Economist Says Tax Hike Won't Derail Recovery \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 DA COSTA'S TAKE\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The idea that the Federal Reserve would commit to keeping interest rates near zero until unemployment fell to at least 6.5% became known as the Evans Rule, because it was first advocated by Chicago Fed President Charles Evans. Now, as the jobless rate hovers just around that level, Mr. Evans appears to be trying take his rule-making inclinations a step further.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In a speech Friday, Mr. Evans defended what he called a "goal-oriented monetary policy strategy." In short, the Fed must clearly lay out the economic objectives it intends to target -- lower unemployment and 2% inflation -- and then proceed to take measures to get there.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 That's already the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's prevailing approach. But with talk of interest rate increases appearing in Fed policy debates, Mr. Evans seems to want to preempt any moves toward rate hikes. Mr. Evans' case was aimed at countering the views of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Stanford University\f28 \uc2 \f2  professor John Taylor, famous for his 1993 "Taylor Rule," which prescribes how central banks should adjust short-term interest rates according to several economic variables.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Mr. Taylor has recently argued the U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has gone too far with its aggressive efforts to spur economic growth, including holding short-term interest rates near zero and buying bonds to lower long-term rates. At a hearing last month following Fed Chairwoman Janet Yellen's congressional testimony, he said "there is little evidence that the policy has helped economic growth or job growth."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Mr. Evans takes issue with this reading. He says the Fed's aggressive support for the economy has helped sustain the recovery and it should continue. He notes unemployment exceeds levels the Fed sees as consistent with full employment while inflation has long been falling short of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's 2% target.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "For me, there is a problem with simplistic approaches. Simple Taylor rules fail the strategic principle to express policy intentions clearly, " Mr. Evans said. The severity of the financial crisis renders mechanistic approaches even less relevant, argued Mr. Evans. "The rule completely breaks down during the Great Recession and its aftermath."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 - By Pedro Nicolaci da Costa\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 MORNING MINUTES: KEY DEVELOPMENTS AROUND THE WORLD\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ECB Watching Ukraine Crisis Closely: Draghi. The \f28 \uc2 \f2 European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  must closely monitor the crisis in Ukraine even though the direct economic impact of the country on the euro zone is limited, ECB President Mario Draghi said Monday, suggesting that policy makers are eyeing the potential for a broader shakeout from the conflict between Ukraine and Russia. \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://on.wsj.com/1ks0ISH"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://on.wsj.com/1ks0ISH}}}\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ukraine\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Troubles Europe's Banks. Escalating tensions over Russian military \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ukraine\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rippled through Europe's banking sector Monday, hitting banks exposed to Eastern Europe. The prices of European bank stocks and bonds tumbled, some banks operating in Ukraine imposed limits on withdrawals from their cash machines, and one bank put on hold the process of selling its Ukrainian unit. \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://on.wsj.com/1fCAG7e"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://on.wsj.com/1fCAG7e}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 Crimea Conflict Makes Poland Rethink Euro, Central Banker Says. Marek Belka, governor of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of Poland\f28 \uc2 \f2 , said Monday the Russian-Ukrainian conflict over Crimea may prompt Poland to rethink its stalled euro-adoption bid. Deeper integration with the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European Union\f28 \uc2 \f2 's single-\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  area may be necessary to anchor stability, he said. \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://on.wsj.com/1g4X3mM"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://on.wsj.com/1g4X3mM}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 What Could Cause a Ukraine Default? Ukraine's economic and political crisis may be heating up, but Kiev still has enough emergency cash reserves to cover its obligations for the next two months. But what would it take to push the country into default? Tensions escalating over Russia's Crimea incursion, Moscow increasing natural gas prices and any delay in international bailout talks, say economists. \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://on.wsj.com/1kPGaR7"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://on.wsj.com/1kPGaR7}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 Follow WSJ coverage the Ukraine-Russia Crisis Here: \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://on.wsj.com/1kSYGZ3"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://on.wsj.com/1kSYGZ3}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 China \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Guides Short-Term Rates Higher. China's money market interest rates jumped on Tuesday after the nation's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  withdrew more liquidity from the financial system, signaling that it intends to continue its efforts to rein in risky lending and facilitate economic restructuring by guiding rates higher. \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://on.wsj.com/1i9EG72"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://on.wsj.com/1i9EG72}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 BOJ Economist Says Tax Hike Won't Derail Recovery. The Japanese economy can withstand headwinds from a coming sales tax increase in April, the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Japan\f28 \uc2 \f2 's chief staff economist, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Eiji Maeda\f28 \uc2 \f2  said in an interview Monday, brushing aside concerns that the higher levy will choke off the nation's recovery. \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://on.wsj.com/1cnG5Vc"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://on.wsj.com/1cnG5Vc}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 Five Reasons Japan's Exports Will Rebound: BOJ's Maeda \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://on.wsj.com/OWiznN"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://on.wsj.com/OWiznN}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 BOJ Gov. Kuroda Warns On Carry Trade. Bank of Japan Gov. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Haruhiko Kuroda\f28 \uc2 \f2  said Tuesday that the country's rock-bottom interest rates are offering investors incentives to conduct yen-carry trade, a risky investment strategy that aims to exploit gaps in countries' interest rates-- Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australia Holds Rates as Jobless Threat Lingers. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Australia's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  kept interest rates on hold for the foreseeable future, saying it expected unemployment to rise beyond its current 10-year high level. \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://on.wsj.com/1n6lO7C"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://on.wsj.com/1n6lO7C}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 IMF's Lagarde: Prolonged Low Inflation May Derail Euro Zone's Recovery. A prolonged period of low inflation in the euro zone may derail the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  area's fragile economic recovery, and must be fought with additional monetary stimulus, \f28 \uc2 \f2 International Monetary Fund\f28 \uc2 \f2  chief Christine Lagarde said Monday. \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://on.wsj.com/1pZXt5X"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://on.wsj.com/1pZXt5X}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 How to Make Draghi's Parliamentary Testimony a Bigger Deal. In a paper released Monday, Bruegel notes European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  President Mario Draghi's exchanges with European parliament lack the policy-related heft of the Fed chief's semiannual testimony to Congress. \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://on.wsj.com/1fVUGGq"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://on.wsj.com/1fVUGGq}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 BOE's Cunliffe: Euro's Existential Crisis Probably Over. The threat of a breakup of the euro zone has receded but the bloc still faces serious economic challenges, Bank of England deputy governor Jon Cunliffe said. He told lawmakers that Mr. Draghi's 2012 pledge to do "whatever it takes" to defend the single \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was successful in assuaging fears that the union would disintegrate. \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://on.wsj.com/1f0PHQf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://on.wsj.com/1f0PHQf}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 G-20 Inflation Eased Again in January. The rise in consumer prices slowed across the world's largest economies for a second straight month in January, driven by falling inflation rates in several large developing economies. \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://on.wsj.com/1hFkPtL"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://on.wsj.com/1hFkPtL}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 Uganda Holds Key Lending Rate. The country's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  kept its key lending rate unchanged at 11.5% for the month of March, citing easing inflationary pressure but warned of potential threats to the economy as foreign donors continue to freeze aid over the recently enacted antigay law. \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://on.wsj.com/1dWV8jL"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://on.wsj.com/1dWV8jL}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 Rubin Says Fed's QE3 Will Lead to Trouble. Former Treasury Secretary Robert Rubin isn't optimistic about U.S. growth prospects this year -- but he doesn't think there is much the Federal Reserve can or should do about it. \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://on.wsj.com/1fVUGGq"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://on.wsj.com/1fVUGGq}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 GRAPHIC CONTENT\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Another sign of weather's drag on the economy is the delay businesses face when trying to get materials. \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://on.wsj.com/1i7Y3gR"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://on.wsj.com/1i7Y3gR}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 FORWARD GUIDANCE\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 - The Senate Banking Committee has postponed, because of snow, the hearing that had been scheduled Tuesday for Fed board nominees Stanley Fischer, Lael Brainard and Jerome Powell. No new date has been announced. \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://on.wsj.com/1eQoWT7"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://on.wsj.com/1eQoWT7}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 - Richmond Fed President Jeffrey Lacker speaks at 4:15 p.m. EST\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 RESEARCH\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Why Were Some Emerging-Market Countries Hit Harder by Taper Talk? Emerging-market nations that fared worst during 2013's so-called "Taper Tantrum" were those whose financial houses were in some level of disorder, a new paper from the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Federal Reserve Bank of San Francisco\f28 \uc2 \f2  argues. \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://on.wsj.com/NO4mbv"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://on.wsj.com/NO4mbv}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://bit.ly/1lvabJ9"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://bit.ly/1lvabJ9}}}\uc2 \f2  The WSJ's Michael Arnold notes alternative analyses of vulnerability factors. \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://on.wsj.com/1djp4d0"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://on.wsj.com/1djp4d0}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 Fed's Bond Buys Aren't as Unconventional as They Appear. The Federal Reserve's bond buys have much the same effect on financial markets, and therefore presumably on the economy, as interest rate cuts, \f28 \uc2 \f2 New York Fed\f28 \uc2 \f2  researchers argue in a blog post. "We conclude that [bond buys] have effects on financial conditions that are very similar to those of more conventional approaches to providing monetary stimulus." \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://on.wsj.com/NoAzq3"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://on.wsj.com/NoAzq3}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://bit.ly/1hZUDIB"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://bit.ly/1hZUDIB}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 COMMENTARY\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Henry Kaufman, a well-known investor and economist, writes in a Wall Street Journal op-ed that he believes the U.S. economy could withstand higher interest rates. "Raising the federal-funds rate by 75 to 100 basis points would be a dramatic initial tightening. But even a new level of 1% to 1.25% is low from an historical perspective." \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://on.wsj.com/1cm1cr2"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://on.wsj.com/1cm1cr2}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 Rather than further loosening its monetary policy, the European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  should make banks write down their bad debts, according to Michael Heise, chief economist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Allianz SE\f28 \uc2 \f2 . \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://on.wsj.com/1gOzgck"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://on.wsj.com/1gOzgck}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 BASIS POINTS\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 - Prices of goods leaving the euro zone's factory gates fell at the fastest annual rate since the end of 2009 in January, adding to concerns that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  area faces a period of low inflation that may hinder its recovery. \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://on.wsj.com/1dWFKnt"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://on.wsj.com/1dWFKnt}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 - Moody's Investors Service projects a corporate default rate of 2.2% this year, or about 61 defaults globally. That would be down from 2.9% last year, when 66 companies reneged on $54.6 billion of loan and bond obligations. \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://on.wsj.com/1eNCtuQ"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://on.wsj.com/1eNCtuQ}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 4 Mar 2014 11:42 EDT \par Grand Central: Evans vs. Taylor -- War of the Fed -2-\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 - Most Gulf Arab countries will see double-digit credit growth this year, marking a strong rebound from the depths of the global recession but also raising budding concern about inflation and the potential for a credit bubble, according to analysts at Moody's Investors Service. \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://on.wsj.com/1omQYX4"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://on.wsj.com/1omQYX4}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 SIGN UP: Grand Central, straight to your inbox on.wsj.com/grandcentralsignup .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 FEEDBACK LOOP: Send us your tips, suggestions and feedback. Write to:Jon.Hilsenrath@wsj.com; Victoria.McGrane@wsj.com; Pedro.daCosta@wsj.com; Michael.Derby@wsj.com; Nell.Henderson@wsj.com; Brian.Blackstone@wsj.com; jason.douglas@wsj.com; Ben.Leubsdorf@wsj.com; Paul.Hannon@wsj.com; Jacob.Schlesinger@wsj.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Follow us on \f28 \uc2 \f2 Twitter\f28 \uc2 \f2 : @WSJCentralBanks, @NHendersonWSJ, @pdacosta, @Blackstonebrian, @PaulHannon29, @michaelsderby, @vgmac, @wsj_douglasj, @BenLeubsdorf, @JMSchels, @ChristophLawton, 