\par\par \b \uc2 European Morning Briefing: Stocks Seen Slightly Higher; Ukraine Stays in Focus\b0\par\par\uc2 4,165 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2014\par 06:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SNAPSHOT:\par \par \uc2 ========\par \par \uc2 U.S. Suspends Trade and Investment Talks With Russia\par \par \uc2 -German Foreign Minister: 'Consequences' For EU-Russia Relations If No Rapid De-Escalation Between Russia, Ukraine\par \par \uc2 -German Foreign Minister: Consequences Could Include Halting Talks On Visa Liberation, EU-Russia Cooperation Agreement\par \par \uc2 -UK Concerned Trade Sanctions Against Russia Could Derail Recovery -Report\par \par \uc2 -Germany's Merkel Walks Fine Line in Russian Standoff\par \par \uc2 -European bourses seen higher, bunds and gilts lower; euro, spot gold down slightly, oil mixed\par \par \uc2 -U.S. Federal Court Rejects BP's Effort To Stop Payouts\par \par \uc2 -Roche Looks to Buy as Its Cash Recovers\par \par \uc2 -Australia Keeps Rates at Record Low\par \par \uc2 -China Money Market Rates Rise After PBOC Withdraws Liquidity\par \par \uc2 -PBOC Chief: Internet Finance-Product Rules Need Strengthening\par \par \uc2 WATCH FOR: EU PPI; U.S. API oil data; U.K. construction PMI\par \par \uc2 UKRAINE:\par \par \uc2 ========\par \par \uc2 Markets in Asia were able to recover as the situation in Ukraine hadn't deteriorated significantly and stocks had already sold-off in the previous session.\par \par \uc2 European foreign ministers backed off any immediate sanctions on Russia on Monday but warned the bloc was ready to act within days if Moscow didn't reverse course.\par \par \uc2 In a statement, the ministers said that "in the absence of de-escalating steps by Russia," the EU would consider suspending talks with Russia on easing visa procedures and on a future trade and economic agreement. The bloc would also consider further targeted measures, they said.\par \par \uc2 Britain is preparing to rule out trade sanctions against Russia amid fears that the Ukraine crisis could derail the global economic recovery, The Daily Telegraph reported Tuesday, citing a government document.\par \par \uc2 But the U.S. said it will suspend talks with Russia aimed at boosting trade and investment and it is drawing closer to imposing economic or political sanctions against Russia.\par \par \uc2 European Council President Herman Van Rompuy confirmed he had summoned EU heads of government to meet in Brussels on Thursday. Officials said the bloc could start to adopt targeted measures then if the situation in Ukraine wasn't calmer. The U.S. Secretary of State John Kerry will be in Kiev Tuesday and an \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  delegation will also be there to meet with Ukraine's interim government and to discuss security and financial issues.\par \par \uc2 EUROPEAN OUTLOOK & U.S./ASIAN SUMMARIES:\par \par \uc2 ========\par \par \uc2 European stock markets may start higher, with bunds and gilts mixed, as the Ukraine crisis simmers.\par \par \uc2 Most Asian markets edged higher on Tuesday, as stocks picked themselves up from a selloff in the previous session sparked by concerns over the situation in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Another sign of easing tension in the market was a softer yen--a safe-haven asset that is typical bought in times of turmoil.\par \par \uc2 "The immediate and likely largest impact from the risk-off sentiment due to the crisis in the Ukraine may have already passed," said Norihiro Fujito, senior investment strategist at Mitsubishi UFJ Morgan Stanley Securities.\par \par \uc2 Wall Street futures were higher, with Treasurys little changed, the euro and spot gold a bit lower, and oil mixed.\par \par \uc2 STOCKS:\par \par \uc2 ======\par \par \uc2 Markets may rebound slightly as investors monitor capital flows among asset classes in Europe.\par \par \uc2 Ahead of the opening, IG has called the FTSE up 17 at 6725, the DAX up 6 at 9365, and the CAC up 14 at 4305.\par \par \uc2 "Why Europe's response is being watched is due to commodity trades with Russia, and unlike its G-7 counterparts in the U.S. and Canada, Europe has a trickier path to tread," noted Evan Lucas, strategist at IG.\par \par \uc2 "The U.S. as expected has acted in accordance with its standard response to these kinds of events. It has cancelled travel for diplomats, threatened sanctions on trade and banking; it has even kicked Russia out of the G-8. But Europe has yet to really push Russia on its current military program."\par \par \uc2 Wall Street futures were slightly higher Tuesday, after all three major U.S. stock indexes posted their biggest one-day declines in a month.\par \par \uc2 But the brunt of market pain was felt by Russia. The country's Micex stock index tumbled 11%, while the Russian ruble plummeted to a record low against the dollar and euro.\par \par \uc2 Investors in the U.S. said they were taking a cautious approach to events in the region. "Most people are of the mind-set that we have to wait and see how this plays out over the next day or two before doing anything with their portfolios," said Joseph Spinelli, head of cash equity trading for the Americas at \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2 . "The belief that it won't escalate to full-on military action seems to be the prevailing wisdom," he said.\par \par \uc2 Susan Bao, co-manager of \uc2 J.P. Morgan\uc2 's U.S. Equity Fund, which oversees about $10 billion said, "beyond the obvious risk of military conflict the most obvious risk for investors are...that Western countries impose economic sanctions on Russia."\par \par \uc2 But Ms. Bao said she remains optimistic on the U.S. economic recovery and is keeping an eye on declines in U.S. stocks for opportunities to add to her portfolio.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 :\par \par \uc2 ======\par \par \uc2 The dollar edged higher in Asia against the euro and other rivals as the Ukraine situation simmered.\par \par \uc2 "At this point, seems like there is very little that the U.S. and EU can do to dissuade Russia from taking this sort of action," said Win Thin, global head of emerging-market \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at \uc2 Brown Brothers Harriman\uc2 . "I don't think this is going to go away or get resolved any time soon," he added.\par \par \uc2 But investors also responded to an uptick in manufacturing data last month as a signal that the U.S. economy is improving, which boosts the dollar.\par \par \uc2 The numbers begin to point to a decreasing "climate effect" on the economy, as they were collected during months where the U.S. experienced bouts of severe weather.\par \par \uc2 Investors will watch closely employment numbers released later this week, including weekly jobless claims on Thursday and the employment situation for February on Friday.\par \par \uc2 As Russia's military secured the Crimean peninsula, its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hit a record low and its stock market plunged in the face of U.S. and European warnings of sanctions over the incursion into Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 BONDS:\par \par \uc2 =====\par \par \uc2 Bunds and gilts may start mixed as improving economic data were largely cancelled out by the political jitters.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy-setting meeting is due Thursday, and economists were speculating whether the bank will act following five months of inflation below 1%.\par \par \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  chief Christine Lagarde on Monday warned that a longer period with low inflation bears the risk of derailing the euro zone's already fragile economy. To fight this, she urged the ECB to launch "even further accommodative policies and targeted measures."\par \par \uc2 On the supply side, Austria and the U.K. have \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  on tap for Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 U.S. Treasurys were little changed Tuesday, after markets rallied as the crisis in Ukraine deepened, unsettling global financial markets and triggering strong bidding for safe-haven assets.\par \par \uc2 The market was "completely geopolitical-driven," said Todd Hedtke, vice president for investment management at the U.S. division of Allianz Investment Management, which manages about $100 billion in assets. "A lot of capital is flowing into Treasurys and other U.S. markets, as the U.S. right now is probably the strongest developed market," he said.\par \par \uc2 Some analysts also noted the latest economic reports show the U.S. economy is emerging from the winter blues, keeping a lid on Treasurys' gains.\par \par \uc2 ENERGY:\par \par \uc2 =======\par \par \uc2 Nymex futures turned mixed in Asia after settling on Monday at their highest level this year. The API Weekly Statistical Bulletin due later today will indicate the status of U.S. oil stockpiles. \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  said oil balances in the U.S. Gulf Coast remain surprisingly tight. The crisis in Ukraine remains in focus for Brent crude, which has risen for two straight sessions although some market participants said concerns of supply disruptions are overblown.\par \par \uc2 Nymex crude was down 1 cents at $104.91/bbl, while Brent was up 8 cents at $111.28/bbl.\par \par \uc2 METALS:\par \par \uc2 ======\par \par \uc2 Base metals traders in Asia were waiting for more news on the crisis in Ukraine and on demand from China. Soft economic data from China along with its sliding \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has hit metals price recently. "If you can't count on demand from China what else is there to prop up copper prices," \uc2 Barclays\uc2  analyst Nicholas Snowdon said.\par \par \uc2 LME three-month copper was down $42 at $6,968.\par \par \uc2 Gold traders faced a similar scenario. "Looking beyond the geopolitical haze, Chinese physical demand remains largely absent, suggesting the current round of gains may retrace should the tension in the Ukraine ease," ANZ said in a report.\par \par \uc2 Spot gold was at $1,350.55/oz, off 6 cents.\par \par \uc2 Write to Dennis Baker at dennis.baker@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 4 Mar 2014 00:00 EDT  European Morning Briefing: DJ Dominant Headlines; Other Top Stories\par \par \uc2 DJ Dominant Headlines:\par \par \uc2 Lotte Shopping Defers up to $1 Billion Singapore IPO\par \par \uc2 Patton Boggs\uc2  Hires Advisers to Aid in Financial Overhaul\par \par \uc2 Obama's Budget Blueprint to Lay Out Policy Goals\par \par \uc2 Transformers Expose Limits In Securing Power Grid\par \par \uc2 Dakota's Oil Producers Say Economics Favor Rail\par \par \uc2 Weaker Yuan Hurts Copper\par \par \uc2 Other Top Stories:\par \par \uc2 U.S. Sues Sprint, Alleging It Overbilled Law Enforcement for Surveillance\par \par \uc2 Sprint Corp\uc2 . overcharged the \uc2 Federal Bureau of Investigation\uc2 , the \uc2 Drug Enforcement Administration\uc2  and other law-enforcement agencies by more than 50% to facilitate eavesdropping on phone calls, the \uc2 U.S. Justice Department\uc2  alleged in a lawsuit filed Monday.\par \par \uc2 PBOC Chief: Internet Finance-Product Rules Need Strengthening\par \par \uc2 China will reinforce regulation of financial products available via the Internet, state-run Xinhua news agency reported.\par \par \uc2 China Money Market Rates Rise After PBOC Withdraws Liquidity\par \par \uc2 China's money market interest rates jumped on Tuesday after the nation's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  withdrew more liquidity from the financial system, signaling that it intends to continue its efforts to rein in risky lending and facilitate economic restructuring by guiding rates higher.\par \par \uc2 Australia Keeps Rates at Record Low\par \par \uc2 Australia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  kept interest rates at a record low as it waits for more signs the economy is emerging from a downturn.\par \par \uc2 Detroit Floats New Pact With Creditors\par \par \uc2 After months of ill-fated deals, the bankrupt city of Detroit has settled with two large banks for about 30 cents on the dollar in the city's only agreement so far with major creditors, according to court filings Monday.\par \par \uc2 Australia Current Account Deficit Narrows\par \par \uc2 Australia's current account deficit narrowed to a seasonally adjusted A$10.14 billion in the fourth quarter of 2013 from the third quarter, the \uc2 Australia Bureau of Statistics\uc2  said Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 U.S. Suspends Trade and Investment Talks With Russia\par \par \uc2 The Obama administration suspended trade and military ties with Moscow and said Monday that punitive sanctions against Russia now are "likely," in a sharply escalating series of actions responding to the Russian military \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 4 Mar 2014 00:00 EDT  European Morning Briefing: Market Prices, Data Calendar\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par DJIA    16168.03 -153.68 -0.94% \par Nasdaq   4277.30  -30.82 -0.72% \par S&P 500  1845.73  -13.72 -0.74% \par FTSE 100 6708.35 -101.35 -1.49% \par DAX      9358.89 -333.19 -3.44% \par CAC40    4290.87 -117.21 -2.66% \par Closing \par  \par Dow Future  16165.00 +17.00 +0.1% \par NASDAQ Future 3673.00 +4.25 +0.1% \par S&P Future    1844.40 +1.30 +0.1% \par As of 0430 GMT \par  \par Nikkei       14641.46 -10.77 -0.07% \par Hang Seng    22591.97 +91.30 +0.41% \par S&P/ASX 200   5399.60 +15.30 +0.28% \par MumbaiSensex 20969.51 +22.86 +0.11% \par Kospi         1957.90  -6.79 -0.35% \par ShanghaiComp  2058.68 -16.56 -0.80% \par Taipei        8576.65 -25.33 -0.29% \par As of 0425 GMT \par  \par USD/JPY 101.65-68 +0.22% \par Range   101.69 101.40 \par EUR/USD 1.3727-30 -0.04% \par Range   1.3739 1.3722 \par GBP/USD 1.6650-54 -0.09% \par Range   1.6669 1.6642 \par U