\par\par \b \uc2 Ukraine Troubles Europe's Banks\b0\par\par\uc2 593 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2014\par 17:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par By Geoffrey T. Smith and Ben Wright \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Escalating tensions over Russian military \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ukraine\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rippled through Europe's banking sector Monday, hitting banks exposed to Eastern Europe.\f28 \par \par \uc2 The prices of European bank stocks and bonds tumbled, some banks operating in Ukraine imposed limits on withdrawals from their cash machines, and one bank put on hold the process of selling its Ukrainian unit.\par \par \uc2 Russian banks suffered the most, with London-listed shares of OAO \uc2 Sberbank\uc2  and OAO VTB, the country's two largest lenders, fell more than 16% and 18% respectively.\par \par \uc2 Foreign banks with \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Russia or Ukraine, including Austria's Raiffeisen International AG, Italy's \uc2 UniCredit SpA\uc2  and France's \uc2 Soci\u233\'00\'E9t\u233\'00\'E9 G\u233\'00\'E9n\u233\'00\'E9rale SA\uc2 , also got clobbered. Raiffeisen, which has 12.1% of its assets in Russia and 3.4% in Ukraine, saw its shares fall about 8%. Shares of \uc2 UniCredit\uc2  and \uc2 Soci\u233\'00\'E9t\u233\'00\'E9 G\u233\'00\'E9n\u233\'00\'E9rale\uc2 , which owns one of Russia's largest retail and corporate banks, both fell about 5%.\par \par \uc2 Raiffeisen said in a statement Monday that it is temporarily halting its planned sale of its Ukrainian unit, Bank Alva. Raiffeisen said it has received multiple offers for the business. The process "is on hold for now, however, due to the situation in Ukraine."\par \par \uc2 UniCredit\uc2 's unit in Ukraine announced on its website that it was limiting cash withdrawals from its ATMs to 1,500 hryvnia, or about $150, per 24 hours. The bank said it was taking the step "in order to provide all the clients with an access to cash money" and that the limits would be removed "in the nearest time with the normalization of the situation."\par \par \uc2 UniCredit\uc2 's move came after \uc2 Ukraine's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  last week imposed limits on withdrawals on foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  accounts, barring customers from withdrawing more than $1,500 or the euro equivalent. There were no \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions on hryvnia accounts.\par \par \uc2 Ukraine's impact on western European banks will be more limited than it would have been in the past, as direct cross-border exposures are less than half their level in 2008, said Elena Romanova, an analyst with Raiffeisen International. Prior to the financial crisis, European banks were chasing market share in what they perceived to be one of the continent's last high-growth markets. However, those banks now hold less than 20% of Ukrainian bank assets.\par \par \uc2 Neither of Russia's top two banks have much of their balance sheets tied up in Ukraine. VTB's $8 billion exposure represents about 3% of its total assets, while \uc2 Sberbank\uc2 's $4.5 billion exposure is 1% of its assets, according to Bob Kommers, an analyst with \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2  in Moscow. The chief executives of Sberbank and VTB said last week that they had suspended new loans to Ukraine but intended to stay in the country over the long term.\par \par \uc2 "But today's price moves are hardly related to Ukraine exposures," Mr. Kommers said. "What's happening today is that people are factoring in higher country risks" for Russia.\par \par \uc2 The market turmoil pushed up Russian banks' costs of market funding. VTB saw the yield on its benchmark notes jump by 0.6 percentage point to 7.25%, according to Tradeweb. The rise is potentially problematic, as VTB gets more than half its funding from the capital markets, Mr. Kommers said.\par \par \uc2 VTB declined to comment on the impact on its business. \uc2 Sberbank\uc2  didn't immediately respond to a request for comment.\par \par \uc2 Ben Edwards contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 March 03, 2014 11:00 ET (16:00 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140303ea33002hz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
