\par\par \b \uc2 JPY Shorts Rise But Ukraine Tensions Cast Doubts -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,780 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2014\par 03:28\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0228 GMT [Dow Jones] Data shows net JPY short non-commercial positions vs USD rose to 85,090 contracts in the week to Feb. 25 vs 79,784 contracts in the week earlier, according to \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. Commodity Futures Trading Commission\f28 \uc2 \f2 's international monetary market commitment of traders report. The data suggest short-term investors such as commodity trading advisors increased their bets against the safe haven JPY. Still, it's unclear whether these investors will continue to bet against the JPY, particularly when geopolitical tensions are growing over the Ukraine situation. (kosaku.narioka@wsj.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0220 GMT [Dow Jones] The New Zealand economy may have grown around 1.0% on quarter in the December quarter, the NZ Treasury said in its monthly economic indicators. "Data released in the past month point to a continuation of the positive momentum seen in the New Zealand economy in the September quarter of 2013, although that 1.4% quarterly growth was boosted by a rebound in agricultural output from the drought earlier in the year," it says. It notes that employment and labour incomes continued to expand firmly and goods exports surged; high visitor arrivals point to an increase in net services exports, and increasing net external migration gains will have boosted aggregate demand. Moreover, "early indications are also positive for the March quarter, with consumer confidence at a seven-year high and job ads increasing in January," Statistics New Zealand is due to report the 4Q data March 20. (rebecca.howard@wsj.com. @FarroHoward)\par \par \uc2 0214 GMT [Dow Jones] Chinese property developers, which are heavily exposed to dollar-denominated debt, are facing higher risks from recent yuan losses. Many of the bigger developers that had offshore fund-raising have benefited from lower financing costs as the yuan appreciated in recent years, \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  says. "The increased \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  volatility may cause a significant increase in medium-term financial cost, which eats into developers' future earnings," it says in a note, adding that if the yuan depreciates 5-15%, reported earnings may decline by 1%-74%, and net gearing may increase by 0.3-21 percentage points. China property stocks have been considered a way to play yuan appreciation but the government has hinted at a more balanced "two-way trading". \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  lists China Overseas Land & Investment (0688.HK), \uc2 China Resources Land\uc2  (1109.HK) and Soho China (0410.HK) among the most heavily exposed to dollar debt. (esther.fung@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0151 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/SGD could be on a path higher in the near term if it ends Monday above 1.2687 and thereby within the daily Bollinger uptrend channel. A confirmation of this bullish technical signal could send USD/SGD past the Ichimoku Cloud resistance zone at 1.2737--which would create even more bullish potential for the greenback. The U.S. dollar is rising in Asia as traders react to the developing Ukraine crisis by offloading risky \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in favor of the safe haven greenback. Adding to pessimism in Asia, the weak China official manufacturing PMI released on Saturday and the disappointing South Korea manufacturing PMI released Monday may also weigh on equities and other assets in the region. USD/SGD is now 1.2683 from its Friday close of 1.2672. (ewen.chew@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0148 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW gaps sharply higher at 1,074.5 from its Friday close of 1,067.5, implying a 0.7% depreciation of the won, as the market reacts to a weekend of risk aversion sparked by a weak China official manufacturing PMI and the crisis in the Ukraine which is threatening to destabilize the western hemisphere. USD/KRW is now enrolled in a higher range of 1,071-1,078, supported by the 20-day Bollinger mid line and capped by the entrance to the Bollinger uptrend channel. The won may experience further weakness if USD/KRW ends the day above 1,078 and thereby within the uptrend channel that could guide the greenback toward 1,084. Economic data released Monday revealed a weakening local economy; South Korea's \uc2 HSBC\uc2  manufacturing PMI dropped to 49.8 in February, versus a 50.9 reading in January, dragging the South Korea stock exchange index down 1.1% and adding to bearishness for the won. USD/KRW is now 1,073.0. (ewen.chew@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0145 GMT [Dow Jones] Safe-haven buying spurs gold prices by more than 1% to $1,343.77/oz in early Asian trading on growing geopolitical concerns in Ukraine. Western governments appeared split on Sunday over how to react to Moscow's military \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine\b0 \highlight\uc2  in what could be the worst breach in East-West relations since the Cold War. "It is an extremely tense situation and we could see gold, JPY, CHF-natural safe-havens-to do well," says Robert Rennie, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Westpac\uc2 . "I can't see a compelling argument for that not to be the case," he notes, adding that there is headroom for prices to rise to $1,360-$1,380/oz, if not the recent highs reached in August-September. Going forward, Rennie expects gold prices to react more sharply during the European session, which will provide a "sense of the quantum of concern" in that region. Spot gold is at $1,340.80/oz, up $12.20 from its previous close. (arpan.mukherjee@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0104 GMT [Dow Jones] The Nikkei down 2.1% at 14,537.24 as the dollar and the euro fall against the yen (now 101.32 and 139.63, respectively) while political upheaval in Ukraine weighs. The latest news has Russia taking control of the Crimean peninsula in Ukraine and appears to be preparing to occupy the territory. "The mood has gone decidedly risk-off, which for the time being will undermine the arguments about how stock fundamentals are looking strong," says CLSA equity strategist Nicholas Smith. "Sure, the tension could be protracted, but it's hardly a tension that significantly involves Japan," he adds. Chris McGuire, manager of Chicago-based hedge fund Phalanx Capital Management says, "The hard floor for the Nikkei stands at about 14,000, so there is actually quite a bit of room for the market to fall before earnest bargain buying can be expected. It may not be prudent to add to a long position at the moment." 32/33 subindexes are lower, with financials and real estate developers taking much of the brunt of the selling, as a weaker dollar lessens the inflationary benefits on their securities and land holdings. \uc2 Sumitomo Mitsui Financial Group\uc2  (8316.TO) is off 2.8% at Y4,415 and Mitsubishi Estate (98802.TO) is down 3.0% at Y2,332. SoftBank (9984.TO) is also down 3.1% at Y7,430. (bradford.frischkorn@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0049 GMT [Dow Jones] Tocom benchmark rubber futures are lower due to concerns over China's growth. The front month Tocom contracts however are slightly higher due to the low-production season. IRCo says in a statement on its website that rubber associations of member countries, as well as Vietnam, are supporting its call to not sell rubber at prevailing low prices. A trader in Thailand says IRCo's statement is supportive for prices, although it's unlikely that it will lift overall market sentiment. Benchmark August Tocom rubber trades Y1.8 lower at Y223.2/kg, off the intraday low of Y222.2/kg.(huileng.tan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2331 GMT [Dow Jones] West Texas Intermediate for delivery in April rises more than 1.3% in early Asian trade, but analysts expect the geo-political tensions in Ukraine to have a wider impact on Brent crude. April WTI earlier hit $103.96/bbl, its highest level since July 2011. "The biggest talking point is Crimea," Evan Lucas, market strategist at IG, says in a report. Western governments appeared split on Sunday over how to react to Moscow's military \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine\b0 \highlight\uc2  in what could be the worst breach in East-West relations since the Cold War. "The market to watch is Brent, which has spiked each time more details of the situation emerge," Lucas says. April Brent crude settled 0.1% higher at $109.07/bbl Friday; it won't start trading again until later in the Asian session. April WTI is at $103.58/bbl, up 99 cents from its previous close. (arpan.mukherjee@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 2212 GMT [Dow Jones] Continued strong New Zealand terms of trade in 4Q13 will support expectations that the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will hike the cash rate in March, providing support for the NZD/USD. Data from Statistics New Zealand shows terms of trade rising for the fourth consecutive quarter, up 2.3% and at its highest level since 4Q 1973. The major contributor to the strong showing is a sharp rise in export volumes, particularly dairy, Statistics New Zealand says. (lucy.craymer@wsj.com, @lucy_craymer)\par \par \uc2 1954 GMT [Dow Jones] The NZD/USD is at 0.8361 early in New Zealand versus 0.8390 late Friday and there is "potential for volatility with plenty of second-tier data out today as well as developments in the Ukraine to watch," says \uc2 Westpac\uc2  Bank Senior \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Strategist Imre Speizer. He notes NZ has 4Q Terms of Trade data while Australia has inventories as well as an inflation indicator. However, "geo-political risk is preventing it (the NZD/USD) from breaking above the key 0.8430 for now." Speizer says there is some support around 0.8345 "but Ukraine is the wild card." (rebecca.howard@wsj.com @FarroHoward)\par \par \uc2 1929 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/JPY is weaker early in New Zealand on geopolitical jitters, says BNZ \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Strategist Raiko Shareef. The USD/JPY is at 101.409, with the yen up around 0.4% in early trade, he says. He expects trading to continue to be dominated by news about Ukraine, in particular after Russia's parliament authorized its president, Vladimir Putin, to use military force in Ukraine, raising fears in the West that Russian forces may move further into Ukraine. Ukrainian Prime Minister Arseniy Yatsenyuk said Sunday that his country was "on the brink of disaster" and personally blamed Putin for bringing the two nations to the verge of war. Overall, the backdrop will be negative for risk assets this morning, Shareef says. (rebecca.howard@wsj.com @FarroHoward)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 March 02, 2014 21:28 ET (02:28 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140303ea3300078\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
