\par\par \b \uc2 GLOBAL MARKETS-Gold, oil rise as  \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine \highlight\uc2  tensions spur safety bids\b0\par\par\uc2 1,120 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2014\par 22:42\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2014 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Stock prices tumble after military \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 * Russia hikes interest rate after ruble slides\par \par \uc2 * Brent crude at 2-month high on fear Putin may cut gas flow\par \par \uc2 * Russian stocks fall 11 pct; European bank shares slump\par \par \uc2 By Herbert Lash\par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, March 3 (Reuters) - Russia's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine\b0 \highlight\uc2  drove up crude oil and prices for gold and government debt on Monday as the heightened tensions spurred investors to seek safe havens and sell any exposure to the region.\par \par \uc2 Crude prices rose more than $2 a barrel, gold futures jumped 2 percent and prices of top-rated euro zone government bonds surged. The aversion to risk took a steep toll on stock markets, with the Moscow bourse slumping 11 percent, wiping nearly $60 billion of value off Russian companies.\par \par \uc2 Stocks across Europe and on Wall Street also took a beating.\par \par \uc2 Market volatility indexes, a sign of investor apprehension, surged, with the Euro STOXX Volatility Index spiking 30.4 percent in its biggest one-day gain since 2011. The U.S. CBOE volatility index surged 20 percent at one point, and ended the session 14.5 percent higher.\par \par \uc2 "Investors had underestimated the risks of an escalation in Ukraine, so the events over the weekend are a wake-up call for the market," said David Thebault, head of quantitative sales trading at Global Equities in Paris.\par \par \uc2 President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 's forces tightened their grip on the Crimea region of Ukraine, sparking the stock plunge in Moscow and forcing \uc2 Russia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to spend $10 billion of reserves to prop up the ruble.\par \par \uc2 Ukraine said Russia was massing armored vehicles on its side of a narrow stretch of water closest to Crimea after Putin declared over the weekend that he had the right to invade his neighbor to protect Russian interests and citizens.\par \par \uc2 The ruble traded off about 1.45 percent after earlier touching record lows against the dollar and the euro. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  lifted its base lending rate by 1.5 percentage points to 7 percent at an unscheduled meeting.\par \par \uc2 Russia's sovereign dollar bonds also fell, while the cost of buying five-year swaps to insure against a Russian debt default jumped 33 basis points.\par \par \uc2 Ukraine's hryvnia \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fell to a record low against the dollar, pushing the country's dollar bonds down 6 points. Safe-haven German Bund futures settled up 76 ticks at 145.14.\par \par \uc2 Banks took the most points off European stock indexes, with lenders exposed to Ukraine and Russia falling sharply. The Euro STOXX banks index fell 3.8 percent in the biggest drop since last August. Austria's Raiffeisen slumped 9.6 percent, while France's \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2  fell 5.4 percent and Italy's \uc2 UniCredit\uc2  lost 6.2 percent.\par \par \uc2 Other companies with significant exposure to Russia also fell, with carmaker Renault shedding 5.4 percent and brewer Carlsberg losing 5.3 percent.\par \par \uc2 The pan-European FTSEurofirst 300 index fell 2.2 percent to close at 1,318.24.\par \par \uc2 No major European stock market escaped the sell-off, with Germany's DAX particularly hard hit, falling 3.4 percent in its biggest single-day drop since May 2012.\par \par \uc2 France's CAC-40 index fell 2.7 percent, and the Italian stock market slid 3.3 percent.\par \par \uc2 "This has shown itself to be a broad risk-off event. Most of the action has been focused on stocks in Europe and the energy sector. A good portion of the market has not reacted to a large degree," said Sam Diedrich, a portfolio manager at Pacific Alternative Asset Management Co. in Irvine, California.\par \par \uc2 "The most likely end-game is a grumpy compromise that allows Ukraine to go its own way in the world while allowing Russia more influence over Crimea," said David Kelly, chief global market strategist at \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  Funds in New York.\par \par \uc2 "If so, uncertainty should fade and the economic data, as the weather improves, should still support a modest over-weight to U.S. stocks over bonds," Kelley said.\par \par \uc2 Putin will not back off but has no need to push further, suggesting markets might soon rebound, said Brad McMillan, chief investment officer at Commonwealth Financial, in Waltham, Massachusetts.\par \par \uc2 "With the substantial downsides and costs of the West trying to reverse it, Europe will probably punt and we will revert to normal faster than anyone expects," McMillan said.\par \par \uc2 The declines in Europe followed overnight weakness in Asia, with MSCI's broadest index of Asia-Pacific shares outside Japan down 0.9 percent and Japan's Nikkei 225 skidding 1.3 percent.\par \par \uc2 On Wall Street, the Dow Jones industrial average closed down 153.68 points, or 0.94 percent, at 16,168.03. The S&P 500 lost 13.72 points, or 0.74 percent, to 1,845.73 and the \uc2 Nasdaq\uc2  Composite dropped 30.818 points, or 0.72 percent, to 4,277.301.\par \par \uc2 For U.S. investors, Russia's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine\b0 \highlight\uc2  comes just as economic data has been expected to improve and provide further upside for stocks on Wall Street, noted \uc2 David Joy\uc2 , chief market strategist at \uc2 Ameriprise Financial\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Data released on Monday showed renewed strength in U.S. manufacturing. But tensions over Ukraine have changed the investment outlook at a time when valuations for U.S. equities are not cheap, Joy said.\par \par \uc2 "Being expensive, it makes sense to me to take some risk off the table and wait to see how this plays out," he said.\par \par \uc2 U.S. factory activity rose in February to its highest level since May 2010, according to financial data firm Markit. Separately, the Institute for Supply Management said its index of U.S. factory activity rose to 53.2 in February, topping expectations.\par \par \uc2 The dollar and yen gained as investors sought the safety of those \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  after Russia's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The greenback was also supported by economic data showing an increase in U.S. personal income and spending in January in the midst of one of the worst winters in recent memory.\par \par \uc2 The dollar index was up 0.47 percent to 80.068. The dollar's gains pushed the euro 0.51 percent lower at $1.3732.\par \par \uc2 Crude oil prices jumped. Brent crude hit a peak of $112.39 a barrel, the highest level since Dec. 30. Brent settled $2.13 higher at $111.20 a barrel. U.S. crude jumped $2.33 to settle at $104.92 a barrel.\par \par \uc2 Gold futures settled up $28.70, or 2.2 percent, at $1,350.3 an ounce.\par \par \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  bond prices rose, with the 10-year note up 16/32 in price to yield 2.6030 percent.\par \par \uc2 Released: 2014-3-6T21:42:10.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020140303ea3300xn6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
