\par\par \b \uc2 REFILE-CEE MARKETS 2-Equities lead fall on concerns over Ukraine\b0\par\par\uc2 1,129 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2014\par 17:16\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2014 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (Adds region in headline)\par     * Banks with branches in Ukraine fall\par     * Forint, zloty drop against euro\par     * Bonds rebound\par     * Leu falls, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Romanian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  soon-dealer\par \par     By Sandor Peto\par     BUDAPEST, March 3 (Reuters) - Equities plunged in Central\par Europe on Monday, also driving the region's main \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par lower, due to worries that Ukraine is drifting towards war with\par Russia.\par     The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Budapest Stock Exchange\f28 \uc2 \f2  led the decline with\par almost 4 percent fall as the shares of Hungary's \f28 \uc2 \f2 OTP Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2 \par , which has some 140 branches in Ukraine, plunged.\par     Moscow stocks fell even more after Russia seized \par Ukraine's Crimea region and tension was escalating between the\par former Soviet states, with no fast solution in sight.\par     "I am certainly going to be defensive towards Central\par European assets, with the risk that financial contagion may\par spread to the other regions under an escalation scenario," said\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Societe Generale\f28 \uc2 \f2  analyst Benoit Anne in a note.\par     The stocks of Russian banks which own units in Ukraine\par plummeted, with \f28 \uc2 \f2 Sberbank\f28 \uc2 \f2  shedding 15 percent. The\par stocks of Western owners of Ukrainian banks also fell.\par     Austria's Raiffeisen shed 8 percent and Italian\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 UniCredit\f28 \uc2 \f2  almost 5 percent. France's \f28 \uc2 \f2 Credit Agricole\f28 \uc2 \f2 \par  and Poland's PKO eased over 3 percent.\par     "It's pretty heavy bloodletting, almost panicky selling\par around the region," an equity trader said in Budapest.\par     The forint and the zloty, the region's\par most liquid units, fell against the euro to their weakest levels\par since deadly clashes in Ukraine two weeks ago, which led to the\par fall of Russian-backed president Viktor Yanukovich.\par     The exports of the region's states to Ukraine are around\par only 1-2 percent of their foreign sales, but a debt default or\par war could have an impact in the region's markets.\par     The Ukrainian crisis has weighed on the region's stock\par markets in the past weeks and the losses deepened on Monday\par because investors are pricing in the worst-case scenarios for\par some stocks, given the Russian military \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ukraine\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par     OTP's shares traded at 3,752 forints at 1533 GMT, down 5.9\par percent, rebounding from a session-low of 3,596.\par     Erste Bank said in a note that a price of 3,300 forints\par would mean that the stock prices in 1,000 forint loss per share\par over Ukraine. One brokerage, Equilor has said that would be the\par worst case scenario, assuming OTP loses all its investments in\par Ukraine - which Equilor called an unlikely scenario.\par     The shares of Hungarian pharmaceuticals firm Richter\par , which has significant sales in both Ukraine and\par Russia, fell 3.3 percent as a weaker Ukrainian hryvnia \par and Russian rouble cause exchange rate losses to the company.  \par     \par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , BONDS HIT\par     The forint has been the most vulnerable to contagion from\par other emerging markets because Hungary is the region's most\par indebted state, its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has cut interest rates sharply\par and the country holds elections in April.\par     The forint traded at 313.12 to the euro at 1528 GMT, down 1\par percent from Friday, but still off last month's lows at 314.80.\par     Some investors tried to push it towards its 324 record lows\par several times last month amid some expectations that a plunge\par could force the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to hike interest rates, like Turkey\par did after a plunge of its own \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par     Polish and Hungarian government bond prices rebounded by\par late trade after sharp early falls. Poland's 2-year yields still\par traded higher by 5 basis points at 3.06 percent.\par     "Nerves over Ukraine have relaxed somewhat, but people are\par still worried that there will be a military conflict there," one\par Budapest-based fixed income trader said.\par     The Polish subsidiary of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Commerzbank\f28 \uc2 \f2 , mBank, warned that any\par escalation of the conflict was likely to hurt a Polish recovery\par that has seen the manufacturing sector expand at the fastest\par pace in more than three years.  \par     "More bans on Polish agricultural products (from Russia) may\par increase the downward pressure on food prices in Poland," the\par bank said. "Interest rate hikes in 2014 seem unlikely now."\par      The leu, whose volatility rose in the past weeks\par due to a break-up of Romania's government, eased 0.4 percent\par against the euro and dealers said it may be already near levels\par where the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  could \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to defend the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par      "I think alarms have rung already. Any potential\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  would be to slow down the pace of depreciation,"\par said one trader in Bucharest.\par     A split in the coalition left the government with a weak\par majority in parliament. Political uncertainty in\par Serbia also lifts the vulnerability of the dinar to\par contagion as the country will hold elections later this month.\par     Dealers said a rise in demand for euro from foreign banks\par lifted turnover in the spot market, and the Serbian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central\par bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , which has been buying the dinar for weeks, continued to\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the market to keep the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  stable.\par     \par                       CEE MARKETS SNAPSHOT AT 1628 CET\par  ************************** \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  ************************\par                              Latest  Previous   Daily   Change\par                              bid     close      change  in 2014\par  Czech crown                 27.338    27.325   -0.05%  -0.02%\par  Hungarian forint           313.120   309.930   -1.02%  -5.16%\par  Polish zloty                 4.194     4.170    -0.57%  -1.05%\par  Romanian leu                 4.518     4.500   -0.40%   -1.28%\par  Croatian kuna                7.649     7.649    0.00%  -0.41%\par  Serbian dinar              115.950   115.800    -0.13%   -1.25%\par  Note: daily change calculated from previous close at 1700 GMT\par  **************************** STOCKS **************************\par                              Latest  Previous   Daily   Change\par                                      close      change  in 2014\par  Prague                      991.44    1015.11   -2.33% +0.46%\par  Budapest                  16992.22  17744.92    -4.24%   -7.32%\par  Warsaw                     2417.07   2518.53    -4.03% +0.53%\par  Bucharest                  6221.40   6435.58   -3.33%   -3.96%\par  ***************************** BONDS ******************