\par\par \b \uc2 Ukraine Tensions Push USD/JPY to One-Month Low, EUR/CHF to One-Year Low -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 864 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2014\par 00:18\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 2318 GMT [Dow Jones] Geopolitical tensions in Ukraine push the safe havens such as JPY and the CHF amid growing speculations that stocks will fall globally, says Atsushi Hirano, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  sales Japan at \uc2 Royal Bank of Scotland\uc2 . Russia's parliament voted unanimously to deploy troops in \b \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine\b0 \highlight\uc2 , defying warnings from Western leaders not to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 . "Tensions have risen with the United States," he says. "Stocks are likely to be negatively affected." The USD/JPY fell to 101.30 -- the lowest in nearly one month -- from 101.80 late Friday in New York. The pair is now at 101.42. The EUR/CHF fell to a one-year low of 1.2102 from 1.2138 Friday. The cross is at 1.2110. (kosaku.narioka@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 2259 GMT [Dow Jones] Strong NZ 4Q terms of trade reflect structural changes in the drivers of New Zealand's key exports and "in particular, increased demand for New Zealand's commodity exports from China and other emerging economies," says \uc2 Westpac\uc2  economist Anne Boniface in a note. Statistics New Zealand said earlier terms of trade in 4Q were up 2.3% on the prior quarter. Boniface adds over the medium term \uc2 Westpac\uc2  is "optimistic that sustained strength in the terms of trade will remain a feature of the New Zealand economic landscape" although \uc2 Westpac\uc2  expects some easing is expected over 2014. (lucy.craymer@wsj.com, @lucy_craymer)\par \par \uc2 2258 GMT [Dow Jones] Australia's S&P/ASX 200 is likely to be restrained by increasing jitters over Ukraine after Russia invaded the former soviet state over the weekend. A relatively muted reaction from risk assets so far Monday suggests the Ukraine will have limited impact on Australian shares, at least until investors can gauge the reaction from European and U.S. markets. China's slightly stronger-than-expected official manufacturing PMI data over the weekend could help the resources sector to some extent. Nevertheless, after rising as much as 8% in the past three weeks, the S&P/ASX 200 has lost momentum near major technical resistance levels and is vulnerable to a substantial fall. While SPI 200 futures rose 0.4% after a positive close on Wall Street, a flat to weaker close is in the cards today. Ex-dividend falls in BHP (BHP, BHP.AU) and AMP (AMP.AU) will detract about 10 points from the index. Last at 5404.8. (david.rogers@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 2240 GMT [Dow Jones] The Nikkei is likely to remain range-bound with a negative bias Monday, as political upheaval in Ukraine weighs on the euro (EUR/JPY now 140.00), and the dollar remains weak (USD/JPY now Y101.57). On Friday, the yuan also marked its largest single-day drop ever against the dollar, as the \uc2 Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  spurred broad selling. \b \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine\b0 \highlight\uc2  mobilized for war on Sunday after Russian President Putin declared he had the right to invade the country in Moscow's biggest confrontation with the West since the Cold War. "The geopolitical unrest and instability in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets will be enough to keep investors on edge and averse to riskier investments such as stocks for now," says SMBC Nikko Securities general manager of equities Hiroichi Nishi. "Stocks' fundamentals remain solid, however, so a big selloff is highly unlikely." He puts the Nikkei's range for the session at 14,750 to 14,950. Nikkei 225 March futures ended Friday's Chicago trading unchanged at 14,920 vs their close earlier in Osaka at 14,860. In the cash market, the Nikkei closed down 0.6% at 14,841.07 Friday. (bradford.frischkorn@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 2241 GMT [Dow Jones] Mild safe haven flows toward the Japanese yen, Swiss franc and the U.S. dollar has featured in early Asia trading as global market assess the risk of growing military tensions between the Ukraine and Russia. Sean Callow, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Westpac\uc2 , said that while the situation is serious, the financial consequences don't appear substantial. He adds that with U.S., Germany not threatening anything more dramatic than not turning up to G8 meetings or maybe some degree of financial sanctions "it seems most investors will view the developments as having limited global impact." According to reports from Washington, with more than 6,000 airborne and naval forces, Russia has taken control of the Crimean peninsula in Ukraine and appears to be preparing to occupy the territory. A closely watched safe cross for safe haven flows is the Australian dollar versus the Swiss franc. The Aussie dollar was under some pressure versus the franc trading at around 0.7830, down from 0.7860 at the start of trade. (james.glynn@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par To contact the Market Talk Editors, \par Bradley Davis, 212 416-2654, bradley.davis@dowjones.com \par Kevin Kingsbury, 212 416-2354, kevin.kingsbury@dowjones.com \par John Shipman, 212 416-2181,john.shipman@dowjones.com \par Patrick Sullivan, 212 416-2326,Patrick.sullivan@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 March 02, 2014 18:18 ET (23:18 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140302ea32000f4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
