\par\par \b \uc2 WTI Jumps 1.3% Early in Asia On Ukraine Tensions; Brent in Focus - IG -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 533 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2014\par 00:31\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2331 GMT [Dow Jones] West Texas Intermediate for delivery in April rises more than 1.3% in early Asian trade, but analysts expect the geo-political tensions in Ukraine to have a wider impact on Brent crude. April WTI earlier hit $103.96/bbl, its highest level since July 2011. "The biggest talking point is Crimea," Evan Lucas, market strategist at IG, says in a report. Western governments appeared split on Sunday over how to react to Moscow's military \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ukraine\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in what could be the worst breach in East-West relations since the Cold War. "The market to watch is Brent, which has spiked each time more details of the situation emerge," Lucas says. April Brent crude settled 0.1% higher at $109.07/bbl Friday; it won't start trading again until later in the Asian session. April WTI is at $103.58/bbl, up 99 cents from its previous close. (arpan.mukherjee@wsj.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 2212 GMT [Dow Jones] Continued strong New Zealand terms of trade in 4Q13 will support expectations that the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will hike the cash rate in March, providing support for the NZD/USD. Data from Statistics New Zealand shows terms of trade rising for the fourth consecutive quarter, up 2.3% and at its highest level since 4Q 1973. The major contributor to the strong showing is a sharp rise in export volumes, particularly dairy, Statistics New Zealand says. (lucy.craymer@wsj.com, @lucy_craymer)\par \par \uc2 1954 GMT [Dow Jones] The NZD/USD is at 0.8361 early in New Zealand versus 0.8390 late Friday and there is "potential for volatility with plenty of second-tier data out today as well as developments in the Ukraine to watch," says \uc2 Westpac\uc2  Bank Senior \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Strategist Imre Speizer. He notes NZ has 4Q Terms of Trade data while Australia has inventories as well as an inflation indicator. However, "geo-political risk is preventing it (the NZD/USD) from breaking above the key 0.8430 for now." Speizer says there is some support around 0.8345 "but Ukraine is the wild card." (rebecca.howard@wsj.com @FarroHoward)\par \par \uc2 1929 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/JPY is weaker early in New Zealand on geopolitical jitters, says BNZ \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Strategist Raiko Shareef. The USD/JPY is at 101.409, with the yen up around 0.4% in early trade, he says. He expects trading to continue to be dominated by news about Ukraine, in particular after Russia's parliament authorized its president, Vladimir Putin, to use military force in Ukraine, raising fears in the West that Russian forces may move further into Ukraine. Ukrainian Prime Minister Arseniy Yatsenyuk said Sunday that his country was "on the brink of disaster" and personally blamed Putin for bringing the two nations to the verge of war. Overall, the backdrop will be negative for risk assets this morning, Shareef says. (rebecca.howard@wsj.com @FarroHoward)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 March 02, 2014 18:31 ET (23:31 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140302ea320009s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
