\par\par \b \uc2 Asian Morning Briefing: Russia Extends Reach in Crimea\b0\par\par\uc2 2,262 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2014\par 23:26\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par MARKETS AT A GLANCE: \par (Data as of approximately 5 p.m. ET Friday) \par                     LAST        CHANGE   % CHG \par DJIA                16321.71    +49.06    +0.30% \par Nasdaq              4308.12     -10.81    -0.25% \par S&P 500             1859.45      +5.16    +0.28% \par Japan: Nikkei 225   14841.07    -82.04    -0.55% \par Hang Seng           22836.96     +8.78    +0.04% \par Shanghai Composite  2056.30      +8.95    +0.44% \par S&P BSE Sensex      21120.12   +133.13    +0.63% \par Australia: S&P/ASX  5404.80      -6.60    -0.12% \par UK: FTSE 100        6809.70      -0.57    -0.01% \par  \par                     PRICE CHG   YIELD% \par U.S. 2 Year          0/32       0.321 \par U.S. 5 Year         -3/32       1.505 \par U.S. 10 Year        -2/32       2.649 \par Australia 10 Year   10/32       4.031 \par China 10 Year       12/32       4.440 \par India 10 Year        5/32       8.857 \par Japan 10 Year        1/32       0.586 \par German 10 Year     -18/32       1.628 \par  \par                            LAST(MID)   CHANGE \par Australia $ (AUD/USD)      0.8930      -0.0034 \par Yen (USD/JPY)              101.78      -0.36 \par S. Korean Won (USD/KRW)    1066.50     -2.84 \par Chinese Yuan (USD/CNY)     6.1481      0.0192 \par Euro (EUR/USD)             1.3801      0.0093 \par WSJ Dollar Index           73.37       -0.27 \par  \par                 LAST      CHANGE   % CHG \par Crude Oil       102.60    0.20     0.20% \par Brent Crude     108.66    0.12     0.11% \par Gold            1326.1    -5.7     -0.43% \par  \par SNAPSHOT: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. stocks ended mostly higher but significantly pared gains as worries over the situation in Ukraine triggered a late-session swoon. Oil ended higher on a last-minute gain. Treasurys and gold settled slightly lower. The euro rose to a 2014 high against the dollar. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par OPENING CALL: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Russia has taken control of the Crimean peninsula in Ukraine and appears to be preparing to occupy the territory, a senior Obama administration official said Sunday. Safe-haven \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  like the yen and the U.S. dollar are benefiting early in Asia as markets remain focused on events in Ukraine.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Investors will also be reacting Monday to China's official February manufacturing data, released over the weekend with a reading of 50.2. Economists polled by the Wall Street Journal on average expected a reading of 50.1, just above the line that separates expansion from contraction. A preliminary gauge released earlier this month by \f28 \uc2 \f2 HSBC\f28 \uc2 \f2  indicated activity had dropped to a seven-month low, catching investors off guard and adding to China growth fears. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par EQUITIES: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. stocks ended mostly higher, with the S&P 500 closing out the best monthly performance since October at a record high.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 However, stocks significantly pared gains as worries over the situation in Ukraine triggered a late-session swoon.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The S&P 500 rose 5.16 points, or 0.3%, to 1859.45, reaching its 48th record closing high over the last 12 months. The index had been up as much as 0.7% at an all-time intraday high of 1867.92 before paring gains.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Dow Jones Industrial Average gained 49.06 points, or 0.3%, to end at 16321.71, but had been up as many as 126.30 points during the session. The Nasdaq Composite Index slipped 10.81 points, or 0.3%, to 4308.12, erasing an early gain of 0.5%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Traders blamed the late-day change in sentiment on news reports saying Russian troops have launched an armed invasion of Ukraine's Crimea region. The veracity of the information couldn't be verified independently by The Wall Street Journal.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "You see headlines like that, and you get a little spooked," especially ahead of the weekend, said Sal Arnuk, co-head of equity trading at brokerage firm \f28 \uc2 \f2 Themis Trading\f28 \uc2 \f2 . "These comments are being read by nervous traders, or computerized traders. If anything, it's an overreaction," Mr. Arnuk said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In corporate news, Jos. A Bank rose 3% after the company rejected \f28 \uc2 \f2 Men's Wearhouse\f28 \uc2 \f2 's $1.8 billion takeover as inadequate, but left the possibility open for an eventual merger. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Men's Wearhouse\f28 \uc2 \f2 's stock advanced 6.7%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's Shanghai Composite gained 0.4% Friday, ahead of the release of China's official manufacturing purchasing managers index for February over the weekend, to end the month with a 1.1% rise. Japan's Nikkei Stock Average fell 0.6% on Friday and lost 0.5% on the month. \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Safe-haven \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  like the yen and the U.S. dollar are benefiting early in Asia as markets remain focused on events in Ukraine.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The euro rose to its strongest level this year against the dollar after the latest inflation readings for the euro zone came in higher than expected, easing pressure on the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to take action when it meets next week.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The single \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rose 0.8% against the dollar to hit $1.3813, its strongest level since Dec. 30. The euro surged past Y140 against the yen and notched gains of 0.3% against the British pound.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The moves stemmed from data showing consumer prices in the euro zone grew 0.8% in February from a year earlier, well below the ECB's target of just below 2%. But they came in higher than expected; the consensus forecast of 24 economists surveyed by The Wall Street Journal last week was for a reading of 0.7%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The release "slightly eases pressure on the ECB to take further stimulative action" at its policy meeting next Thursday, said Howard Archer, chief U.K. and European economist at IHS Global Insight. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par BONDS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A minor pullback in Treasury prices left the market with enough gains on the month to post another set of positive returns, countering widespread expectations at the start of the year that 2014 would be one of rising yields.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Benchmark 10-year notes declined 3/32 in price to yield 2.653%, according to Tradeweb. That yield is flat from where it started the month but down from 3% when the year began. The 30-year bond gained 3/32 to yield 3.589%, while two-year notes were unchanged to yield 0.325%. Bond yields move inversely to their prices.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Losses were steeper earlier in the session, before reports of Russia sending troops into Ukraine's pro-Russian region of Crimea fueled a late-day rush toward safer assets.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The deteriorating situations in Ukraine and Venezuela have given [Treasurys] a supportive bid," said Brian Svendahl, co-head of U.S. fixed income at RBC Global Asset Management. "Near term, we think rates stay in this lower range with geopolitical uncertainty and emerging-market weakness providing support, along with the weak data." \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par COMMODITIES: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Oil futures ended with a small gain, recovering from a decline earlier in the session on expectations for frigid weather in much of the U.S. to boost demand for heating oil.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Light, sweet crude for April delivery settled up 19 cents, or 0.2%, at $102.59 a barrel on the \f28 \uc2 \f2 New York Mercantile Exchange\f28 \uc2 \f2 . Brent crude on the ICE futures exchange climbed 11 cents, or 0.1%, to $109.07 a barrel.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Oil futures have climbed for two of the past three sessions as well as for two of the past three months. Strong demand for heating oil has helped support oil prices as an unusually frigid winter gripped the central and eastern U.S. in recent months. Colder-than-average weather is forecast to last into mid-March.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Weather continues to have a strong influence on the oil markets, having buoyed crude-oil prices in the U.S.," said \f28 \uc2 \f2 Barclays\f28 \uc2 \f2  analyst Miswin Mahesh.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Gold futures fell as a record-breaking rally in U.S. stocks sapped investor demand for haven assets. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par TODAY'S HEADLINES: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Russia Preparing for 'Occupation' of Crimea, U.S. Says\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 With more than 6,000 airborne and naval forces, Russia has taken control of the Crimean peninsula in Ukraine and appears to be preparing to occupy the territory, a senior Obama administration official said Sunday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 West Is Split Over Response to Russia's Ukraine Foray\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Western governments appeared split Sunday over how to react to Moscow's military \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ukraine\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in what could be the worst breach in East-West relations since the Cold War, as they discussed what appeared to be limited options to counter Russia.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China Calls for Dialogue to Resolve Ukraine Crisis\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 BEIJING - China called for dialogue to resolve the fractious situation in Ukraine, though Beijing did not criticize Russia outright for sending troops into Ukraine and threatening further use of force.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 J. Crew in Talks to be Sold to Japan's \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fast Retailing\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 J. Crew Group\f28 \uc2 \f2  is in talks to sell the clothing retailer to Japan's \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fast Retailing\f28 \uc2 \f2 , owner of the Uniqlo apparel chain, for as much as $5 billion, said a person familiar with the matter.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Mt. Gox Files for Bankruptcy Protection\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bitcoin exchange \f28 \uc2 \f2 Mt. Gox\f28 \uc2 \f2  said it was filing for bankruptcy protection after losing almost 750,000 of its customers' bitcoins, marking the collapse of a marketplace that once dominated trading in the virtual \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Morgan Stanley\f28 \uc2 \f2  Taps Laroia as Asia Co-CEO\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Morgan Stanley\f28 \uc2 \f2  has appointed Gokul Laroia as Asia co-CEO. Laroia, former head of Asia mergers & acquisitions at the Wall Street bank, will share the title with Beijing-based Wei Sun Christianson, who has held it since 2011.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. GDP Cut to 2.4% as Growth Slows\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The U.S. economy's momentum is slipping as bad weather and overseas volatility restrain consumers and businesses, diminishing hopes for an early-2014 breakout in growth.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Regulators Probing How Goldman, Citi Allocated Bonds\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Securities regulators have opened an inquiry into the way \f28 \uc2 \f2 Goldman Sachs Group\f28 \uc2 \f2 , \f28 \uc2 \f2 Citigroup\f28 \uc2 \f2  and other banks allocate new bond issues among investors, people familiar with the matter said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. End-February Consumer Sentiment Rises Above Expectations\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. consumers ended February with little change in their assessment about the economy. The Thomson-Reuters/\f28 \uc2 \f2 University of Michigan\f28 \uc2 \f2  final February sentiment index edged up to 81.6 from a preliminary reading of 81.2, the same level posted at the end of January.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. Pending Home Sales Index Edges Up\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The National Association of Realtors said its index of pending home sales rose 0.1% in January from the prior month to 95. That was less than economists' forecast of a 1.4% increase but the first rise since June.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Citi Revises Results, Hit by Mexico Fraud\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2 Mar 2014 17:26 EDT \par Asian Morning Briefing: Russia Extends Reach in -2-\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Citigroup\f28 \uc2 \f2  said it is cutting its fourth-quarter and full-year results by about $235 million after allegedly being duped by fraudulent billings at its Mexico unit.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Blackstone CEO Had 2013 Payout of $465.4 Million\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Stephen Schwarzman, co-founder and chief executive of Blackstone Group, took home about $465.4 million in dividends, compensation, and fund payouts for 2013, according to a regulatory filing.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Sears Says Reviewing Systems but No Sign of a Breach\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Sears Holdings\f28 \uc2 \f2  said it is "actively reviewing" its computer systems amid reports of hacking in the retail industry but so far has found no evidence of a breach. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par RECENT DJ DOMINANTS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 No Signs of Stocks' Froth in the Tea Leaves\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Pfizer\f28 \uc2 \f2  Pushes to Sell Over-the-Counter Version of Lipitor\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Winton Capital\f28 \uc2 \f2  Plans Expansion Drive With Up To 100 New Hires By Year-End\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. Aid Chief Says Myanmar Can't Stall on Reforms\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Durban Defends Marc Andreessen and \f28 \uc2 \f2 eBay\f28 \uc2 \f2  CEO Donahoe - Interview\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Goldman Trader Who Led Fabrice Tourre's Group Leaves The Bank\f28 \par \p