\par\par \b \uc2 Australian Morning Briefing: Dollar, Yen Strengthen as Ukraine Jitters Weigh\b0\par\par\uc2 3,101 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2014\par 21:26\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par GLOBAL STOCK INDEXES: \par DJ Indus     16321.71  +49.06 +0.30% \par Nasdaq        4308.12  -10.81 -0.25% \par NYSE Comp    10425.86  +28.05 +0.27% \par S&P 500       1859.45   +5.16 +0.28% \par Russell 2000  1183.03   -4.91 -0.41% \par TSM          19637.68  +34.94 +0.18% \par S&P/TSX      14209.59   -5.15 -0.04% \par FTSE 100      6809.70   -0.57 -0.01% \par FTSE 250     16726.00 +202.98 +1.23% \par Xetra DAX     9692.08 +103.75 +1.08% \par CAC40         4408.08  +11.69 +0.27% \par Nikkei Stock 14841.07  -82.04 -0.55% \par Hang Seng    22836.96   +8.78 +0.04% \par S&P/ASX 200   5404.80   -6.60 -0.12% \par NZSX-50       4990.04  +25.70 +0.52% \par Global Stock Indexes at 0425 GMT Saturday. \par  \par INTERBANK \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREIGN EXCHANGE\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  RATES: \par           Latest    Previous  % Chg \par                     Close \par USD/JPY  101.74-80  101.74-80  0.00 \par EUR/USD  1.3800-06  1.3800-06 +0.00 \par GBP/USD  1.6743-48  1.6743-48  0.00 \par USD/CHF 0.8794-800 0.8794-800  0.00 \par USD/CAD  1.1059-70  1.1059-70  0.00 \par AUD/USD  0.8928-34  0.8928-34  0.00 \par NZD/USD 0.8377-404 0.8377-404 +0.00 \par Interbank \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Foreign Exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Rates at 1950 GMT. \par  \par TREASURYS: \par 2Y   99 27/32 flat  0.325% flat \par 5Y   99 30/32 -4/32 1.511% +2.7BP \par 10Y 100 27/32 -3/32 2.653% +1.1BP \par  \par US STOCKS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. stocks ended mostly higher Friday, with the S&P 500 closing out the best monthly performance since October at a record high.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 However, stocks significantly pared gains as worries over the situation in Ukraine triggered a late-session fall.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The S&P 500 rose 5.16 points, or 0.3%, to 1859.45, reaching its 48th record closing high over the last 12 months. The index had been up as much as 0.7% at an all-time intraday high of 1867.92 before paring gains.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Dow Jones Industrial Average gained 49.06 points, or 0.3%, to end at 16321.71, but had been up as many as 126.30 points during the session.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Nasdaq Composite Index slipped 10.81 points, or 0.3%, to 4308.12, erasing an early gain of 0.5%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The S&P 500 rose 4.3% in February, the fifth monthly gain in six months. The Dow closed Friday 1.5% below its Dec. 31 record closing high of 16576.66, but its 4% monthly gain was the largest since January 2013.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Traders blamed the late-day change in sentiment on news reports saying Russian troops have launched an armed invasion of Ukraine's Crimea region. The veracity of the information couldn't be verified independently by The Wall Street Journal.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "You see headlines like that, and you get a little spooked," especially ahead of the weekend, said Sal Arnuk, co-head of equity trading at brokerage firm \f28 \uc2 \f2 Themis Trading\f28 \uc2 \f2 . "These comments are being read by nervous traders, or computerized traders. If anything, it's an overreaction," Mr. Arnuk said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The S&P 500 and Dow slipped into negative territory during the final hour of trading, before bouncing back into positive territory ahead of the close.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Data out Friday helped support the belief that recent weak data was temporary. The Institute for Supply Management's Chicago-area purchasing managers index for February rose from a month earlier, bucking economists' forecasts for a decline. In addition, the Thomson-Reuters and \f28 \uc2 \f2 University of Michigan\f28 \uc2 \f2  consumer-sentiment index for February unexpectedly rose.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Separately, the Commerce Department's downward revision of its U.S. gross domestic product estimate for the fourth quarter, to 2.4% from an initial 3.2%, matched economists' predictions, and a measure of upcoming home sales rose less than economists expected.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Lawrence Creatura, portfolio manager at Federated Investors, which oversees $376.1 billion, said the market's drift higher reflects an economy that is improving steadily, though somewhat grudgingly. "You can't deny things are improving, for the economy and the market," Mr. Creatura said. "The slope is upward. It's a yawner, but it's still positive."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In corporate news, Jos. A Bank rose 3% after the company rejected \f28 \uc2 \f2 Men's Wearhouse\f28 \uc2 \f2 's $1.8 billion takeover as inadequate, but left the possibility open for an eventual merger. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Men's Wearhouse\f28 \uc2 \f2 's stock advanced 6.7%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 NII Holdings\f28 \uc2 \f2  tumbled 55% on heavy volume after the wireless service provider warned of liquidity issues, saying it would need to significantly improve its performance and consider options to raise liquidity to meet financial obligations in 2015 and beyond.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Deckers Outdoor Corp\f28 \uc2 \f2 . slumped 12% after the maker of Ugg and Teva brand footwear provided an earnings growth outlook late Thursday for the current fiscal year that was below analyst projections. That overshadowed fiscal fourth-quarter earnings and revenue that topped estimates. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par WORLD \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The euro is under pressure against the dollar in early trading in New Zealand Monday on concerns about Ukraine and after China manufacturing data, Stuart Ive, Wellington-based adviser at OM Financial, said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Heavily armed units believed to be Russian soldiers effectively seized control of Ukraine's Crimean peninsula--home to Russia's Black Sea fleet--over the past several days. On Saturday, Russia's parliament authorized Russian President Vladimir Putin to use military force in Ukraine, raising fears in the West that Russian forces may move further into the country.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The euro will get squeezed as traders look to safe havens," such as the dollar, the yen and the Swiss franc, Mr. Ive said. The euro is at $1.3757 early in New Zealand.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The yen is benefiting from its safe-haven status amid the geopolitical jitters, BNZ \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Strategist Raiko Shareef said. The dollar is at Y101.326.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Mr. Shareef expects trading to continue to be dominated by news about Ukraine, in particular after Russia's parliament authorized the use of military force in Ukraine. Ukrainian Prime Minister Arseniy Yatsenyuk said Sunday that his country was "on the brink of disaster" and personally blamed Mr. Putin for bringing the two nations to the verge of war.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Overall, the backdrop will be negative for risk assets this morning, Mr. Shareef said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In data over the weekend, a closely watched gauge of China's manufacturing activity dropped to an eight-month low in February, the latest sign of a slowdown in the country's factory sector.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The government's official purchasing managers index fell to 50.2 in February from 50.5 in January, where any number of more than 50 indicates expansion. The reading was more than the 50.1 median forecast of 11 economists polled earlier by The Wall Street Journal, and indicates growth at a marginal pace. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par US TREASURYS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A minor pullback in Treasury prices Friday left the market with enough gains on the month to post another set of positive returns, countering widespread expectations at the start of the year that 2014 would be one of rising yields.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Benchmark 10-year notes declined 3/32 in price to yield 2.653%, according to Tradeweb. That yield is flat from where it started the month but down from 3% when the year began. The 30-year bond gained 3/32 to yield 3.589%, while two-year notes were unchanged to yield 0.325%. Bond yields move inversely to their prices.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Losses were steeper earlier in the session, before reports of Russia sending troops into Ukraine's pro-Russian region of Crimea fueled a late-day rush toward safer assets.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The deteriorating situations in Ukraine and Venezuela have given [Treasurys] a supportive bid," said Brian Svendahl, co-head of U.S. fixed income at RBC Global Asset Management. "Near term, we think rates stay in this lower range with geopolitical uncertainty and emerging-market weakness providing support, along with the weak data."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Indeed, alongside events abroad causing bouts of anxiety, the outlook on the U.S. economy has turned murky as well. Data over the past month have mostly been discouraging as this season's particularly harsh winter stalled factory activity, dampened retail sales and hurt housing demand.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Friday's selloff in Treasurys was limited even though the latest reports turned a bit rosier. One release showed an unexpected rise in Chicago's regional manufacturing activity, while another showed consumer sentiment holding up this month.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Until incoming data paint a clearer picture of the U.S.'s growth prospects, bond traders expect a continuing appetite for safe-haven Treasurys keeping the 10-year yield between 2.6% and 2.8%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Longer-term, however, many still expect growth to pick up later this year and lead the 10-year yield to more than 3% by the end of 2014. That outlook goes hand in hand with expectations that the Fed will push ahead with its stimulus reduction-another force that should push yields higher.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We strongly believe that as we approach mid-March, we will be close to a turn in economic data," said the rates strategy team at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of America\f28 \uc2 \f2 Merrill Lynch\f28 \uc2 \f2 , recommending investors start betting against Treasurys again. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par EUROPEAN STOCKS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Better-than-expected U.S. data lifted European stock markets out of the red on Friday, capping the strongest month for the benchmark index since July.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The region's indexes had traded with losses earlier in the day after better-than-expected euro-zone inflation data was seen as easing pressure on the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to cut rates at its policy meeting in the coming week.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Stoxx Europe 600 index rose 0.2% to close at 338.02, ending the month 4.8% higher. On the week, the benchmark scored a 0.6% gain.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Shares of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Serco Group PLC\f28 \uc2 \f2  led the list of gainers, surging 12% after the London-listed government-services company appointed \f28 \uc2 \f2 Aggreko PLC\f28 \uc2 \f2 's \f28 \uc2 \f2 Rupert Soames\f28 \uc2 \f2  as chief executive from June 1. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Aggreko\f28 \uc2 \f2  dropped 4.3%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Old Mutual PLC\f28 \uc2 \f2  climbed 5.9% after the investment firm lifted its full-year dividend and said adjusted operating pretax profit rose last year on a constant \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  basis.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 On a more downbeat note, shares of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Pearson PLC\f28 \uc2 \f2  slumped 5.9% after the publisher of the Financial Times said full-year operating profit declined due to weakness in its U.S. education business.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2 Mar 2014 15:26 EDT \par Australian Morning Briefing: Dollar, Yen -2-\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 More broadly, Friday's main event in Europe was the inflation report for February, surprisingly showing that the pace of price increases remained at 0.8%. Economists worried inflation would drop as low as 0.6% amid speculation such a trend would push the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to further loosen policy to stave off the threat of deflation. After the data, however, analysts said ECB President Mario Draghi now has more breathing space and faces less pressure to cut interest rates.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The odds are stacked in favor of no further reaction from the ECB after this inflation number. The most we could get could be a little nudge on the forward guidance," said Naeem Aslam, chief market analyst at Ava Trade, in emailed comments.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Investors continued to keep a close eye on Ukraine, where Russian soldiers took over Crimean airports and the acting interior minister described it as "armed invasion and occupation."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Among regional indexes, Germany's DAX 30 rose 1.1% to 9,692.08, ending with a gain of 0.4% for the week and 4.1% for all of February. France's CAC 40 gained 0.3% to 4,408.08. It rose 0.6% on the week and advanced 5.8% in February. The U.K.'s FTSE 100 index closed flat at 6,809.70, dropped 0.4% on the week, but posted a 4.6% monthly gain. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par ASIAN STOCKS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Asian stocks were mixed Friday but recorded solid gains for February, rebounding from losses during the first month of the year.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The broad MSCI Asia ex-Japan index was up 2.6% for the month until Thursday, as stocks recovered from a selloff in January that was caused by a wide range of concerns over emerging markets including China and Turkey. Although some issues remain in the emerging world, with political uncertainty in Ukraine and the recent sharp fall in the Chinese yuan, the worry in the markets appears to have abated.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We started off the year with too much optimism and that left markets vulnerable to a correction," said Shane Oliver, head of investment strategy and chief economist at AMP Capital in Sydney. "Investors are now taking a more discerning view of the emerging world and that has allowed share markets to rebound."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Hong Kong's Hang Seng Index was 3.6% higher month-to-date after a 5.5% loss in January, while South Korea's Kospi rose 2%. Japan's Nikkei Stock Average, however, was down 0.5% for the month and down 8.9% year-to-date.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 On Friday, stocks were mixed with a slight downward bias as the yuan fell the most since China ended its peg to the dollar in 2005, dropping 0.9% to a 10-month low.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Chinese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  had been in focus all week as it reversed its long-term appreciation, with signs that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is preparing to allow the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to be more freely traded intraday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's Nikkei lost 0.6% to 14841.07 as the yen strengthened during Asian trade, after the country posted upbeat economic data for January--such as rising industrial production and improvements in the labor market.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Elsewhere in Asia, South Korea's Kospi was up 0.1%, and Singapore's Straits Times Index was up 0.5%. In China, the Shanghai Composite rose 0.4% and Hong Kong's Hang Seng Index was flat.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Markets were waiting for the release of China's official manufacturing Purchasing Managers' Index for February on Saturday. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par NEW ZEALAND STOCKS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The NZX-50 closed up 0.5%, or 25.7 points, at 4990.04 as a positive lead from U.S. equity markets added to a number of local stocks being buoyed by the index, Forsyth Barr broker Suzanne Kinnaird said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Air New Zealand\f28 \uc2 \f2  rose 0.6% after it reported a good first-half result Thursday, she said. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Metlifecare\f28 \uc2 \f2  closed 0.2% lower after paring back early losses following its first-half result. Ms. Kinnaird said that while the result was soft it was in line with expectations. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par AUSTRALIAN & NZ CORPORATE & ECONOMIC CALENDAR: \par LOCAL TIME/GMT \par 1045/2145  NZ   Jan International Visitor Arrivals \par 1045/2145  NZ   4Q  Overseas Trade Indexes (Volumes / Prices) provisional \par 0930/2230  AUS  Feb Australian PMI \par 1030/2330  AUS  Feb \f28 \uc2 \f2 TD Securities\f28 \uc2 \f2  Monthly Inflation Gauge \par 1130/0030  AUS  4Q Business Indicators \par 1130/0030  AUS  Feb ANZ Job Ads \par 1630/0530  AUS  Feb Commodity Price Index \par N/A        AUS  Rex Minerals Limited (RXM.AU) Interim 2014 Results \par N/A        AUS  Aurora Oil & Gas Ltd (AUT.AU) Full year 2013 Earnings conference call \par N/A        NZ   Auckland International January Monthly Traffic Update \par N/A        AUS  Horizon Oil Limited (HZN.AU) Interim 2014 Results \par  \par LONDON METAL EXCHANGE: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Base metals on the London Metal Exchange closed mixed Friday, with nickel closing higher, still benefiting from the confusion over an ore export ban in Indonesia.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 At the close of open-outcry trading in the London ring, LME three-month copper was 0.3% down on the day at $7,005 a metric ton. Three-month aluminum fell 0.8% to $1,753 a ton.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Copper is set to finish down on the week, with demand for the red metal dwindling," said IG market analyst David Madden.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The Chinese authorities are trying to keep a lid on the property market, and traders aren't bullish about Beijing's manufacturing figures set to be released this weekend," he added.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Nickel closed 1.9% higher on the day at $14,700 a ton.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Analysts at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Commerzbank\f28 \uc2 \f2  said in a note to clients earlier in the session that the metal's conspicuous gains were "doubtless on the back of the still unclear export situation in Indonesia," where an ore export ban was implemented in January.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Having asserted on a number of occasions in recent weeks that it will apply the ore export ban as planned and without any exceptions, the government is now back-pedalling after all on the issue of the progressive export tax," the German bank said. "Indonesia will remain a headache for the metals markets for quite some time yet." \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par US COMMODITIES: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Oil futures ended Friday with a small gain, recovering from a decline earlier in the session on expectations for frigid weather in much of the U.S. to boost demand for heating oil.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Light, sweet crude for April delivery settled up 19 cents, or 0.2%, at $102.59 a barrel on the \f28 \uc2 \f2 New York Mercantile Exchange\f28 \uc2 \f2 . Brent crude on the ICE futures exchange climbed 11 cents, or 0.1%, to $109.07 a barrel.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Gold futures fell as a record-breaking rally in U.S. stocks sapped investor demand for haven assets. Gold for April delivery, the most active contract, closed down $10.20, or 0.8%, at $1,321.60 a troy ounce on the Comex division of the \f28 \uc2 \f2 New York Mercantile Exchange\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Wheat prices rose 2.9%, the biggest gain in three weeks, amid concerns that unrest in Ukraine will slow grain exports from the country and that cold weather will hurt U.S. output. Wheat for March delivery at the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Chicago Board of Trade\f28 \uc2 \f2  rose 16.75 cents to $5.99 a bushel, notching the biggest percentage increase since Feb. 4. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par TOP STORIES: \par Russian Troops Encircle Ukrainian Military Base in Crimea \par NATO Chief Says Russia Must Stop Military Activities in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ukraine\f28 \b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ukraine\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Puts Military on Full Alert After Russian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Threat \par U.K. Pulls Out Of G8 Preparatory Meetings In Russia \par China PMI Shows Slowdown \par Mexican Police Question \f28 \uc2 \f2 Citigroup\f28 \uc2 \f2  Employee Over Alleged Fraud \par Stabbing Attack in China Leaves 29 Dead, 130 Injured \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Berkshire Hathaway\f28 \uc2 \f2  Profit Rises on Improving Economy \par Almost Half A Billion Worth Of Bitcoins Vanish \par China's Geely Acquires U.K. Electric-Car Startup \par Gap Plans to Expand in China \par U.S. Economy Expands at Sharply Slower Pace \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Moody's\f28 \uc2 \f2  Lifts Outlooks for Germany, Austria, Luxembourg \par MPs Shot as Protesters Storm Libya's Parliament \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 March 02, 2014 15:26 ET (20:26 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140302ea320002s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
