\par\par \b \uc2 GLOBAL MARKETS-World stocks dip, Wall St flat; ruble at 5-yr low\b0\par\par\uc2 1,115 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Februar 2014\par 22:44\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2014 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par * European shares close lower as luxury goods makers slide\par     * S&P 500 closes flat; retailers strong\par     * China's yuan steady after Tuesday's slide, ruble hits\par 5-year low\par     * Gold touches four-month peak\par     * Fed Chair Yellen to testify to Senate on Thursday\par \par \par     By Chuck Mikolajczak\par     NEW YORK, Feb 26 (Reuters) - World stock markets dipped on\par Wednesday, stung by growing worries over some emerging markets\par as escalating tensions in Ukraine sent the Russian ruble to a\par five-year low, while equities on Wall Street ended flat.\par     The drop in the ruble came a day after China's yuan had its\par biggest drop in three years, which weighed on shares of European\par luxury goods makers because of their heavy exposure to emerging\par markets.\par     The S&P 500 saw gains fade in the latter portion of trading,\par failing in its latest attempt to break its record closing high\par of 1,848.38 set on Jan. 15. Data showing sales of new U.S.\par single-family homes surged to a 5-1/2-year high in January eased\par concerns about a slowing of economic momentum, but was not\par enough to lift the benchmark S&P index over what has proven to\par be a strong resistance level. \par     "It just seems like there's an absence of buyers once we hit\par this area. There's no firm commitment to drive the S&P back\par above this particular level," said Todd Salamone, Senior Vice\par President of Research at Schaeffer's Investment Research in\par Cincinnati, Ohio. \par     The ruble slid as tensions escalated in Ukraine after\par Russian President \f28 \uc2 \f2 Vladimir Putin\f28 \uc2 \f2  ordered drills by his armed\par forces to test combat readiness in western Russia, near the\par border with Ukraine. \par     The threat of debt default by Ukraine also increased. Russia\par holds $3 billion worth of Ukrainian debt issued last December,\par which could end up in default if certain terms are breached.\par     Ukraine has asked the \f28 \uc2 \f2 International Monetary Fund\f28 \uc2 \f2  to help\par prepare a new financial aid program, while the country's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central\par bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  chairman said the new government would soon have its own\par anti-crisis program ready.  \par     The United States warned Russia it would be a "grave\par mistake" to embark on a military \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ukraine\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and\par said Washington was considering $1 billion in U.S. loan\par guarantees for Kiev. \par     The ruble was at 36.025 to the dollar, after touching\par its lowest level since March 2009. Ukraine's hryvnia hit\par a record low of 10 per dollar.\par     The market moves come as some investors have already been\par pulling money out of emerging markets and putting it back into\par better-understood developed economies.\par     Chinese shares and the yuan  stabilized after\par sharp falls on Tuesday, although dealers suspect the \f28 \uc2 \f2 People's\par Bank of China\f28 \uc2 \f2  was maintaining a gradual squeeze on the yuan\par , to inject more two-way volatility into the market\par and wrong-foot speculators betting it would keep rising.\par     The country's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  regulator said a dip in the\par yuan is normal as some investors unwind their long bets on the\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , helping inject two-way exchange rate volatility over\par time. \par     \par     On Wall Street, retailers were a bright spot for a second\par straight session, with the S&P retail index up 1.4\par percent. \par     Shares of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Lowe's Cos Inc\f28 \uc2 \f2 , the No. 2 U.S. home\par improvement retailer, rose 5.4 percent to $50.72 after the\par company reported strong growth in quarterly sales, showing that\par it was narrowing the gap with market leader \f28 \uc2 \f2 Home Depot Inc\f28 \uc2 \f2 \par . \par     \f28 \uc2 \f2 Target Corp\f28 \uc2 \f2  shares climbed to an almost six-week\par high after the company reassured investors that customers were\par beginning to return to its U.S. stores, suggesting that the\par lingering impact of a data breach that affected millions of\par shoppers may not be as bad as some had feared. Shares of Target\par jumped 7 percent to $60.49. \par     The Dow Jones industrial average rose 18.75 points or\par 0.12 percent, to 16,198.41, the S&P 500 gained 0.04\par points to stay virtually flat, to 1,845.16 and the Nasdaq\par Composite added 4.477 points or 0.1 percent, to\par 4,292.064.\par     Gains on Wall Street were held in check ahead of testimony\par by Federal Reserve Chair \f28 \uc2 \f2 Janet Yellen\f28 \uc2 \f2  before the \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. Senate\f28 \uc2 \f2  on\par Thursday. She is likely to get questions on the recent spate of\par soft U.S. economic news and what it might mean for policy. \par     "We have a new Fed Chair on Capitol Hill tomorrow, and\par there's just that hesitancy to push stocks up into these new\par highs," said Salamone.   \par     The MSCI world equity index, which tracks\par shares in 45 nations, fell 0.9 point or 0.22 percent, to 407.44.\par     The pan-European FTSEurofirst 300 closed down 0.2\par percent at 1,348.75 points, weighed down by declines in luxury\par goods makers. \par     Shares of \f28 \uc2 \f2 LVMH\f28 \uc2 \f2  finished down 1.6 percent, Kering\par  fell 2.2 percent, and Hermes was down 0.2\par percent. The weakness was attributed to a note from \f28 \uc2 \f2 Credit\par Suisse\f28 \uc2 \f2  analysts who downgraded the sector to "benchmark" from\par "overweight," citing the sector's big exposure to China and\par other emerging markets.\par     \f28 \uc2 \f2 Credit Suisse\f28 \uc2 \f2  was also in the spotlight, down 0.7\par percent as a \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. Senate\f28 \uc2 \f2  subcommittee alleged new misdeeds by\par the Swiss lender. \par     The dollar rose to its highest level in two weeks against a\par basket of major \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  as investors sought safety on the\par geopolitical tensions in Russia and Ukraine. \par     The dollar index rose 0.3 percent to 80.503 after\par hitting a high of 80.524, it strongest level since February 13.\par     The euro fell against the dollar, down 0.44\par percent at $1.3684. Against the yen, the dollar was up 0.14\par percent at 102.36.\par     U.S. Treasuries, the benchmark for global borrowing costs,\par rose 10/32 in price on the benchmark 10-year note to\par yield 2.665 percent. \par     Gold retreated after earlier on Wednesday hitting four-month\par highs as the dollar advanced and the new home sales data dented\par bullion's safe-haven appeal. Spot gold earlier touched\par its highest level since Oct. 30, at $1,345.35 an ounce before\par falling 0.9 percent while U.S. COMEX gold futures for\par April delivery settled down $14.70 at $1,328 an ounce. \par  \par     Brent crude rose 1 cent to settle at $109.52 a\par barrel and U.S. oil settled up 76 cents at $102.59, after\par government data showed a surprisingly small build in crude\par inventories and another large drawdown at the American\par benchmark's delivery point.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Released: 2014-3-1T21:44:54.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020140226ea2q00dwc\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
