\par\par \b \uc2 Bid to End Ukraine Crisis Stumbles\b0\par\par\uc2 By Alan Cullison   \par \uc2 725 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Februar 2014\par 22:37\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 KIEV--Prospects for ending Ukraine's political crisis looked dim on Tuesday, as the president's allies and critics couldn't agree on moves toward creating a coalition government ahead of meetings with a top European official.\par \par \uc2 President Viktor Yanukovych's opponents in Parliament failed to drum up support for a plan to weaken presidential powers and distribute some to the prime minister and his cabinet. Although both sides say shuffling Mr. Yanukovych's powers might resolve the crisis, the measure failed to get support from his allies, who hold a majority in the chamber.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Yanukovych will meet on Wednesday with the \uc2 European Union\uc2 's foreign-policy chief, Catherine Ashton, who arrived in Kiev on Tuesday evening and held talks with opposition leaders. But Foreign Minister Leonid Kozhara said the Ukrainian president wouldn't make any decisions on forming a new government or ceding power until after Friday, when he will travel to Russia to meet with President Vladimir Putin as the Olympic Games begin in Sochi.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Yanukovych's government, meanwhile, appeared tepid toward a financial bailout the U.S. and Europe are dangling as an impetus for the president to strike a compromise with critics who took to the streets last year to protest his decision to scrub a planned deal with the EU in favor of a $15 billion financial-aid package from Russia.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Kozhara said the government has heard no official explanation of such a plan, which is being cobbled together by Washington and European governments and would require Mr. Yanukovych to appoint a technocrat government.\par \par \uc2 Assistant Secretary of State Victoria Nuland is set to arrive in Kiev on Thursday to urge the government and opposition to proceed with overhauls in exchange for a short-term aid package that would support an interim government as it works with the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  on implementingtough economic measures.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Yanukovych faces a delicate balancing act as he discusses a financial deal with the West. Russia delivered $3 billion of its promised aid in December, but last week held up a $2 billion tranche after the Ukrainian president dismissed his pro-Russian prime minister and his cabinet in a bid to pacify his opponents, who continue to man barricades in central Kiev. Russia's state-controlled media has cast the opposition in Ukraine as dominated by violent radicals, and the Kremlin has complained that the West is fanning the conflict by supporting Mr. Yanukovych's opponents.\par \par \uc2 The Ukrainian government is now close to running out of money. Revenue is down sharply due to protests, which have seized parts of the country from government control.\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, \b \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the Ukrainian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the hryvnia, which last week suffered its biggest one-day loss since 2009 because of heavy selling. The government had projected that the economy could return to growth this year after officially staying flat in 2013, but analysts say those hopes are all but dashed.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Yanukovych has offered opposition leaders the prime minister's job and limited number of posts in the cabinet, but they have so far refused, saying they would be little more than figureheads.\par \par \uc2 But Mr. Yanukovych and his allies have said changing the constitution would be a long and laborious process. His opponents accuse him of stalling tactics. In Parliament on Tuesday, opposing factions blamed one another for lack of progress.\par \par \uc2 Opposition leader Arseniy Yatsenyuk said Parliament can't end the crisis because Mr. Yanukovych's Party of Regions "doesn't have a will to."\par \par \uc2 Vladislav Lukianov, a leader of the party in Parliament, said he and others are ready to work out a change to the constitution, but that rushing through with it would only create more conflicts. The constitution "needs to be corrected and it needs to be done in a legal way," he said.\par \par \uc2 In talks with opposition leaders on Tuesday, Mr. Yanukovych said such changes could take as long as six months to negotiate, said opposition party leader Vitaly Klitschko.\par \par \uc2 "The country has a political crisis, we don't have a half a year to wait, " Mr. Klitschko said.\par \par \uc2 Katya Gorchinskaya contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Write to Alan Cullison at alan.cullison@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 February 04, 2014 16:37 ET (21:37 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140204ea24003wf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
