\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine \highlight\uc2  PM: National Bank to Limit  \highlight22\uc2 Currency Interventions \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 By Kjetil Malkenes Hovland   \par \uc2 381 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 November 2012\par 10:37\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global Equities News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 OSLO--\b \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Prime Minister Mykola Azarov said Friday he would like to continue a loan program with the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2 , but that the IMF demand to raise household gas prices was unreasonable.\par \par \uc2 "The loan program and the overall cooperation program with the IMF continues," Mr. Azarov told Dow Jones Newswires. "The time of the expiration of this particular program is December this year. It is naturally in our wishes to continue this program. We expect that a new program can be developed."\par \par \uc2 Speaking at the Norwegian prime minister's guest house, Mr. Azarov said his country had paid back more than $3 billion on previous IMF loans in 2013. "We would very much like to renew the program, there's no doubt about that," he said, adding that Ukraine will hold meetings with the IMF on Dec. 6.\par \par \uc2 "The IMF mission of experts is about to arrive in Kiev in December," he said. "We shall be working together this time."\par \par \uc2 The IMF put a previous $15 billion loan program on hold last year after Ukraine backtracked on promises to increase the gas price for households. Gas subsidies add to Ukraine's budget deficit. Mr. Azarov agreed with the IMF's assessment of the country's deficit, but said the demand was unreasonable.\par \par \uc2 "We have just one point where we can't find any compromise," he said. "Here, I refer to the unreasonable requirement to raise [gas] tariffs flat. We believe the major positions for the IMF is the budget deficit and the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves stability. These are the major indicators for them. But the means the government undertakes to reach these objectives are domestic affairs."\par \par \uc2 He said the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had been forced to spend its reserves to defend the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as the recent parliamentary election, heavily criticized by his opponents, led to a substantial increase in the demand for \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "If the national bank failed to spend resources to support the peg, then the uncontrolled devaluation of the national \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  could have happened," said Mr. Azarov.\par \par \uc2 Write to Kjetil Malkenes Hovland at kjetilmalkenes.hovland@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 Subscribe to WSJ: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://online.wsj.com?mod=djnwires"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://online.wsj.com?mod=djnwires}}}\uc2  [ 30-11-12 1003GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020121130e8bu000h1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
