\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 2-Ukraine hryvnia hits 3-year low amid devaluation talk\b0\par\par\uc2 683 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 November 2012\par 09:37\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Dealers speculate \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  could devalue to 8.40 to dollar\par \par \uc2 * Any depreciation expected to be gradual\par \par \uc2 * Cbank \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  for first time since Nov. 5\par \par \uc2 (Adds cbank \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 KIEV, Nov 14 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine\b0 \highlight\uc2 's national \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the hryvnia, tumbled to a three-year low of 8.27 to the dollar on Wednesday amid widespread expectations of a devaluation, possibly to a level of 8.40, according to dealers.\par \par \uc2 With last month's election out of the way, market players expect the authorities, who have kept the hryvnia pegged at around 8 to the dollar since early 2010, to allow some depreciation soon to plug widening trade and current account deficits and secure a resumption of IMF lending.\par \par \uc2 "There have been practically no offers for sales of dollars. Everybody is expecting a fall to about 8.40," said one dealer at a major bank.\par \par \uc2 Though the hryvnia touched 8.27/8.30 during trading, according to dealers, it ended at 8.25/27 on Wednesday after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 , offering to sell dollars at 8.05 hryvnias per dollar.\par \par \uc2 Analysts polled by Reuters this month predicted that the hryvnia would dive to 9 to the dollar in 2013 with authorities no longer needing to waste foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves on keeping the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at its current pegged rate while the trade deficit widens.\par \par \uc2 These analysts saw the hryvnia trading at 8.40 per dollar by the end of 2012.\par \par \uc2 Before Wednesday's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had not stepped in to support the hryvnia since Nov. 5 when it offered to sell dollars again at 8.05 hryvnias. Its absence since then had raised speculation it is ready to allow the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  much more flexibility, although any devaluation is expected to be gradual.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials have been at pains to play down talk of a devaluation.\par \par \uc2 "We had an unusual month in October linked to the elections. All national elections are accompanied by a surge in net demand for foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  ... At the end of the elections, demand usually subsides," Sergiy Korablin, a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  official, said on Tuesday at a conference in Kiev.\par \par \uc2 In a sign of the authorities' concern at the outflow of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Ukraine's parliament voted on Nov. 6 to give the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  legal powers to force exporters to convert at least part of their foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  earnings into hryvnias. The law requires President Viktor Yanukovich's signature before it can come into effect.\par \par \uc2 "The National Bank is not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  because the authorities clearly want to resume cooperation with the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2 , which is recommending a more flexible hryvnia," said Olexander Okhrimenko, head of the Ukrainian analytical centre, an independent research house.\par \par \uc2 "We can even expect a strengthening of the hryvnia if compulsory sales of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  earnings by exporters are introduced and IMF credit is resumed," Okhrimenko said.\par \par \uc2 The IMF, Ukraine's key lender, froze its $15 billion aid programme for the former Soviet republic last year after Kiev, wary of a political backlash, chose not to raise household gas and heating prices as recommended by the Fund.\par \par \uc2 The trade deficit widened to$9.3 billion in January-September 2012 from $6.2 billion in the same period of 2011, according to \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  data. Analysts attributed the rising deficit mostly to declining steel exports and the high price of gas imports.\par \par \uc2 Analysts, who do not expect a quick recovery in global demand for metals, have raised their trade deficit forecast for 2012 to $13.60 billion, from $11.65 billion in a Reuters poll last month. They see the trade deficit at $12.15 billion next year, compared with $10.2 billion in 2011.\par \par \uc2 Ukraine's current account deficit widened to $9.276 billion in January-September 2012, from $5.895 billion in the same period last year.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Natalia Zinets; Writing By Richard Balmforth; Editing by Susan Fenton)\par \par \uc2 UKRAINE-HRYVNIA/ (UPDATE 2) | ABN | D | E | RBN | M | ABX | BNX | FUN | AFA | CSA | LBY | RWSA | RWS | REULB | GNS | RNP | SXNA   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020121114e8be000d5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
