\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Ukraine  \highlight22\uc2 forex \highlight\uc2  reserves fall to $26.8 bln in Oct\b0\par\par\uc2 296 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 November 2012\par 10:36\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Adds background)\par \par \uc2 KIEV, Nov 7 (Reuters) - Ukraine's foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves fell to $26.812 billion as of Oct. 31 from $29.255 billion on Sept. 30, the State Statistics Service said on Wednesday.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the local interbank market regularly, selling dollars to keep the hryvnia pegged at about 8 per dollar.\par \par \uc2 The hryvnia has come under pressure from the former Soviet republic's widening trade and current account deficits as European demand for steel - Ukraine's key export - dwindled due to the euro zone crisis.\par \par \uc2 Some market players expected the authorities to allow some hryvnia depreciation after President Viktor Yanukovich's Party of the Regions and its allies retained a majority in the Oct. 28 parliamentary election.\par \par \uc2 But Ukraine's parliament voted on Tuesday to give the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  legal powers to force exporters to convert at least part of their foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  earnings into hryvnias, indicating the peg may remain in place.\par \par \uc2 Non-deliverable forwards showed the hryvnia weakening to 9.28/9.58 per dollar in six months, from 8.19 on Wednesday, and 10.30/10.50 in a year's time.\par \par \uc2 Ukraine repaid $407 million to the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  on Oct. 30, according to IMF data, although it was not clear which part of that money had come from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Ukraine is due to repay another $1 billion to the Fund this month and its latest IMF programme has been frozen since early 2011 due to Kiev's refusal to implement unpopular reforms such as raising household gas and heating prices.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Olzhas Auyezov; Editing by Ruth Pitchford)\par \par \uc2 UKRAINE-RESERVES/  (UPDATE 1) | ABN | D | M | RNP | PTD   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020121107e8b7000ts\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
