\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine \highlight\uc2  allows hryvnia to strengthen slightly\b0\par\par\uc2 284 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Juni 2010\par 17:59\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Abandons \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate maintained for two months\par \par \uc2 * Follows IMF advice\par \par \uc2 By Natalia Zinets\par \par \uc2 KIEV, June 9 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Wednesday adjusted its \b \highlight22\uc2 forex intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate for the third consecutive day, showing increased hryvnia <UAH=> exchange rate flexibility during an IMF mission visit.\par \par \uc2 The International Monetary Fund has earlier urged Ukraine to allow greater exchange rate flexibility and take other steps to curb inflation and cut budget deficit. Ukraine is seeking a $19 billion loan from the IMF.\par \par \uc2 This week, as the Fund's mission arrived in Kiev to discuss economic policies, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  lowered its dollar bid <NBU01> to 7.9185 from 7.9250 in a series of adjustments after keeping the rate unchanged for almost two months.\par \par \uc2 "President (Viktor Yanukovich) said last week that Ukraine is switching to a flexible exchange rate policy," said a trader at a local bank.\par \par \uc2 "Why not show ... the president and the IMF that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is ready for a flexible exchange rate?"\par \par \uc2 Another trader said there had been no changes in fundamentals -- strong export revenues that support the hryvnia.\par \par \uc2 "Export revenues keep coming but one can not talk about a sharp increase there," he said.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been buying about $1 billion a month on the market since March, replenishing its reserves.\par \par \uc2 Ukraine's key export sectors of steel and chemicals took a battering in the global downturn and the Yanukovich administration says a new IMF credit programme will help to get the economy back on to its feet and to restore competitiveness. (Reporting by Natalia Zinets; Writing by Olzhas Auyezov; Editing by Ruth Pitchford)\par \par \uc2 UKRAINE-HRYVNIA/|LANGEN|ABN|M|D   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020100609e6690014s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
